(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-judiciary"]={"plain":{"bills":{"judiciary":{"slug":"judiciary","title":"The Judiciary Integrity Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short Title","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the Judiciary Integrity Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"Congress finds the following:\n\n1. Public confidence in the judiciary depends on the assurance that a judge deciding a case has no financial stake in its outcome. That assurance is presently supplied by disclosure and by recusal, and both can be strengthened without impairing judicial independence.\n\n2. Congress has long required financial disclosure by Federal judges under the Ethics in Government Act, has required prompt reporting of securities transactions, and has required online public access to judicial financial disclosure reports. This Act builds upon that established framework rather than displacing it.\n\n3. Reporting has repeatedly shown that judges have participated in matters involving parties in which they held a financial interest, contrary to 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455. The cause is more often the absence of a reliable mechanism to detect a conflict than any intent to disregard the obligation. A conflict-screening system that compares a judge's disclosed holdings against the parties to each assigned matter addresses that cause directly.\n\n4. A judge who holds only diversified, non-issuer-specific assets has no financial stake in any particular litigant and faces no recusal question arising from a holding. Making that arrangement available, and making a judge's decision to adopt it publicly visible, allows the benefit of the arrangement to be obtained by consent rather than by command.\n\n5. Nothing in this Act diminishes the compensation of any judge, and nothing in this Act is intended or shall be construed to affect the tenure of any judge, which is governed by Article III and by impeachment alone.\n\n6. **Enumerated powers and constitutional limits.** Congress enacts this Act pursuant to Article I, Section 8, Clause 9, and Article I, Section 8, Clause 18, as applied to the establishment and administration of the courts, and pursuant to its established authority to require financial disclosure and to govern disqualification under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455. The application of \u00a7 6-10 and \u00a7 6-11 to an individual who has access to non-public information of the United States in the performance of an arrangement with the judicial branch is enacted pursuant to Article I, Section 8, Clause 3, and as necessary and proper to the administration of the courts through such arrangements. Congress does not enact, and this Act does not contain, any provision diminishing judicial compensation, altering judicial tenure, or vesting authority over judges in any officer of the executive branch.\n\n7. A wager held by a judge or judicial employee, or by an individual who has access to the judiciary's non-public information in performing an arrangement with it, on the acts, decisions, or outcomes the Government will produce \u2014 including the outcome of litigation \u2014 is a stake adverse to the public's, and it injures confidence in the courts whether or not any decision is affected. A market in such wagers gives every insider an edge the public cannot hold, and the edge follows access rather than the form of employment, which is why the prohibition of \u00a7 6-10 reaches every officer and employee of the judicial branch and every individual with such access; wagering regulated by the States on athletic contests and games of chance, where no judicial officer holds an insider's edge by reason of office, is left to the States."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, AND DEFINITIONS","body":"For purposes of this Act:\n\n**(a) \"Covered judge\"** means a Justice of the Supreme Court of the United States, a judge of a United States court of appeals, a judge of a United States district court, a judge of the Court of International Trade, and a judge of any other court established under Article III of the Constitution.\n\n**(b) \"Other judicial officer\"** means a United States bankruptcy judge, a United States magistrate judge, a judge of the United States Court of Federal Claims, a judge of a court established under Article I of the Constitution, and any other judicial officer of the United States who is not a covered judge, to the extent provided in \u00a7 4-6.\n\n**(c) \"Eligible asset\"** means: cash and cash equivalents; obligations of the United States; a diversified index fund or exchange-traded fund tracking a published broad-market index and applying no screen; a diversified lifecycle or target-date fund composed of such funds; a fund offered under Section 3 of the People's Prosperity Act, or an interest in the People's Prosperity Fund established under Section 2 of that Act; a primary or secondary residence; a tangible personal item held for use and not for investment; and an interest in a defined-benefit plan.\n\n**(d) \"Family member\"** means the spouse or domestic partner of a covered judge and any dependent child, as those terms are used in the Ethics in Government Act.\n\n**(e) \"The Conference\"** means the Judicial Conference of the United States.\n\n**(f) \"The Administrative Office\"** means the Administrative Office of the United States Courts.\n\n**(g) \"Electing judge\"** means a covered judge who has made and not been released from an election under Section 2.\n\n**(h) \"Automated system\"** means a computational process that produces an output bearing on the disposition of a matter. The term applies whatever the method by which the output is produced, whether or not the process is described as artificial intelligence, whether or not it derives its behavior from data, and whether or not a person could produce the same output by other means."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. Financial Disclosure","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND CONFLICT SCREENING","body":"**(a)** Each covered judge shall file the reports required by the Ethics in Government Act, and shall do so subject to the requirements of this Section. This Act does not reduce any obligation imposed by that Act or by any other law.\n\n**(b) Assets with a readily ascertainable market price.** Each asset having a readily ascertainable market price \u2014 including a security, a fund interest, a digital asset, a deposit, and a like holding \u2014 and each liability, valued above $5,000, adjusted for inflation from the date of enactment, shall be reported at its fair market value as of the reporting date, stated as a point estimate and not as a range or category. For such an asset, fair market value is that market price. An item valued at or below that amount may be reported in aggregate.\n\n**(c) Assets without a readily ascertainable market price.** An asset having no readily ascertainable market price \u2014 including real property other than a residence, an interest in a private business or partnership, a work of art, and a collectible or other tangible object held for investment \u2014 shall be reported only if its fair market value is reasonably believed to exceed $100,000, adjusted for inflation from the date of enactment. An asset reportable under this subsection shall be valued by independent appraisal upon first reporting and upon any material change in the asset or its value; in an intervening year, the judge may report a good-faith updated estimate stating the date and basis of the most recent appraisal. No appraisal is required of an asset not reportable under this subsection.\n\n**(c-0) Electing judges.** For a judge who has made an election under Section 2, the threshold in subsection (c) applies only to an asset the judge may hold under that election. An asset the electing judge is required to convert under \u00a7 2-2 shall be identified regardless of value, together with a good-faith estimate of value, without independent appraisal below that threshold.\n\n**(d) Scope.** Subject to the thresholds in subsections (b) and (c), the report shall identify each asset, attributed asset, entity, trust, beneficial interest, foreign holding, digital asset, and liability of the covered judge and of each family member, on the standard of attribution applicable under the Ethics in Government Act.\n\n**(d-1) Spousal and family employment.** The report shall identify the employer of each family member, the nature of the family member's work, and, where the family member's work consists of advocacy, consulting, lobbying, or advisory services bearing on Federal litigation, regulation, or policy, each client for whom such services were provided during the reporting period. Where identification of a client is barred by a legal obligation of confidentiality, the judge shall so state and shall identify the client to the Administrative Office for use in conflict screening under \u00a7 1-3, which shall not publish it.\n\n**(d-2) Where the family member does not supply the information.** Where a family member declines or is unable to supply a client identity, the judge shall so state, shall identify what was asked and of whom, and shall record the response. Non-supply is disclosed, not passed over in silence. The certification under subsection (e) governs a statement made under this subsection.\n\n**(d-3) Practices consisting principally of such services.** Where a family member's practice consists wholly or principally of advocacy, consulting, lobbying, or advisory services of the kind described in subsection (d-1), each client is reportable under that subsection without a separate judgment whether the particular engagement bears on Federal litigation, regulation, or policy.\n\n**(d-4) Services provided through an entity.** Subsections (d-1) through (d-3) reach services provided through a corporation, partnership, limited liability company, or other entity to the same extent as services provided by the family member directly, including where the entity rather than the family member holds the client relationship.\n\n**(e) Certification.** Each report shall carry the covered judge's certification, made to the best of the judge's knowledge after reasonable inquiry, that the judge made good-faith efforts to obtain the information reported and asked each family member to provide information within the categories reported.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND CONFLICT SCREENING","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Transaction Reporting","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND CONFLICT SCREENING","body":"**(a)** A covered judge shall report each acquisition, disposition, or exchange of an asset or attributed asset above a de minimis amount set in the schedule to this Act, within 45 days, as required by existing law, and the Administrative Office shall publish each such report within 48 hours of filing in the database under Section 3.\n\n**(b) Custodial reporting and auto-population.** Each custodian or brokerage holding an account of a covered judge or an attributed asset shall report that account, and each transaction in it, directly to the Administrative Office in structured, machine-readable form. From that data the Administrative Office shall generate a draft of each report required under subsection (a) and present it to the judge for confirmation or correction. A judge satisfies the reporting obligation by timely confirming or correcting the draft, and a judge is not liable for an error in a draft arising from data reported by a custodian, provided the judge timely confirms or corrects it and has not failed to disclose an asset or account.\n\n**(b-1) Identification precedes the duty.** The Administrative Office shall furnish each custodian, on a secure channel, the information necessary to determine whether an account is subject to subsection (b). A custodian's duty under subsection (b) attaches only as to an account so identified. No custodian is required to determine whether an account holder is a covered judge or whether an asset is attributed.\n\n**(b-2) The party who knows, tells.** Each covered judge shall identify to the Administrative Office each custodian or brokerage holding an account of the judge or an attributed asset, and shall report a change within 30 days.\n\n**(b-3) No public roster.** Information furnished under subsection (b-1) passes from the Administrative Office to the custodian on a secure channel and is not published. This Section requires no list of covered judges or family members to be made public and authorizes no custodian to disclose it.\n\n**(b-4) An account may not be declined by reason of this Section.** No custodian may decline to open or to maintain an account, close an account, or vary its terms, fees, or availability, by reason of a duty imposed by this Section or by reason of an account holder being a covered judge or a holder of an attributed asset. **No cost of compliance with this Section may be charged to the account or to the account holder.** This subsection binds the custodian's conduct."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Conflict Screening","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND CONFLICT SCREENING","body":"**(a) Screening system.** The Administrative Office shall establish and maintain an automated conflict-screening system that compares the disclosed holdings of each covered judge, and of each family member, against the parties, parents, subsidiaries, and affiliates in each matter before that judge or in which the judge is to participate, and against the entities identified in each corporate disclosure statement filed in that matter.\n\n**(a-1) The screen runs against the data that exists.** The comparison under subsection (a) runs against each corporate disclosure statement as filed. Where a filed statement does not identify subsidiaries and affiliates, the system shall record that the comparison was limited to the entities disclosed, and that limitation shall appear in any notice under subsection (b) and in any record under subsection (b-1).\n\n**(b) Notice.** The system shall notify the judge and the clerk of court of any match before the judge takes action in the matter, and shall record the notice.\n\n**(b-1) What a notice of no match is, and is not.** A notice that no match was found is a record that no match was found in the data screened. It is not a determination that no disqualifying interest exists, and it neither satisfies nor bears upon the judge's obligation under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455.\n\n**(b-2) Availability of the system.** Where the system is unavailable, the judge may act in the matter. The comparison shall be run and any notice issued as soon as practicable thereafter. This Section conditions no judicial action on the operation of a computer system.\n\n**(c) Duty unchanged.** The obligation to disqualify remains governed by 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455. This Section supplies a mechanism for identifying a disqualifying interest; it does not alter the grounds for disqualification, the judge's duty, or the judge's discretion where the statute confers it.\n\n**(d) Reporting of matches.** The Administrative Office shall publish annually the aggregate number of matches identified, the number resulting in recusal, the number of matches in which the judge participated without recusal, and the number resolved by divestiture or by waiver where permitted by law, without identifying any matter in which recusal was not required.\n\n**(e) An electing judge.** A judge who has made an election under Section 2 and holds only eligible assets has no issuer-specific holding to match. The screening system shall record that status, and the judge is relieved of no other obligation under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455.\n\n**(f) The system relieves no party of its own duty.** Nothing in this Section relieves a party of any obligation to identify a conflict or to make any disclosure required by law or by rule, and the availability of the system is not a ground for excusing a failure to do so."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3a","heading":"\u00a7 1-3A. Annual test of reconciliation and of screening","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 DISCLOSURE AND CONFLICT SCREENING","body":"**(a) Reconciliation.** The Administrative Office shall test annually whether the reports filed under this Section reconcile with the custodial reports received under \u00a7 1-2(b), applying a published method, and shall publish the results in aggregate without identifying any individual.\n\n**(b) The screening is tested, not assumed.** The Administrative Office shall test annually whether the conflict screening required by \u00a7 1-3 was in fact performed in each matter to which it applied, and shall publish the proportion of matters in which it was performed and the proportion in which a match was returned.\n\n**(c) The model is one this Act already uses.** The test required by this Section is conducted in the manner of the audit under \u00a7 7-4(c), applying a published statistical standard and publishing any material deviation.\n\n**(d) Within the judiciary.** The test is performed by the Administrative Office and by no other body. **Nothing in this Section confers authority on any officer outside the judicial branch to review, approve, or act upon a filing, a screening, or a recusal decision.**\n\n**(e) No merits review.** A test under this Section determines nothing about the correctness of any recusal decision and is not a finding that any provision has been violated."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Election","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE VOLUNTARY ELECTION","body":"**(a)** Any covered judge may elect to be bound by the holding requirements of this Section. The election is available to a judge in service on the date of enactment and to a judge who takes office thereafter, on identical terms.\n\n**(b)** No covered judge is required to make an election. A judge who does not elect is subject to Section 1 and to existing law, and to nothing further under this Act. Nothing in this Act, and no action of the Conference, the Administrative Office, or any officer of the United States, shall condition the appointment, assignment, compensation, tenure, promotion, caseload, or treatment of any judge upon making or declining to make an election.\n\n**(c)** An election is made by filing a written declaration with the Administrative Office in the form prescribed by the Conference.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE VOLUNTARY ELECTION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Effect of Election","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE VOLUNTARY ELECTION","body":"**(a) Holding requirement.** An electing judge shall hold only eligible assets, and shall not hold an interest in an individual issuer, a private company, a partnership interest, a commodity, a digital asset, or a hedge, private-equity, or venture fund.\n\n**(b) Family.** An election extends to assets of the electing judge's spouse or domestic partner and dependent children, on the standard of attribution applicable under the Ethics in Government Act, except that the earned income and employment benefits of a family member, a family member's actively operated business, and assets a family member holds independently and to which the judge has no access or economic benefit are not reached.\n\n**(c) Conversion period.** An electing judge shall convert non-eligible assets that are readily marketable within 12 months of election, and non-eligible assets that are not readily marketable within 48 months, holding them during that period under an arrangement providing no participation in management, and recusing from any matter affecting them.\n\n**(d) Involuntary acquisition.** An asset received by inheritance, bequest, court judgment, or bona fide family gift is disclosed within 30 days and converted within the periods in subsection (c), measured from receipt.\n\n**(e) Tax treatment.** A conversion required by an election under this Section is eligible for deferral of capital gains under the Certificate of Divestiture procedure of 5 C.F.R. Part 2634, Subpart J, and 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 1043.\n\n**(f) Hardship.** The Conference shall provide a hardship exception under published standards, addressing at a minimum an asset that cannot be sold without destroying its value or a family enterprise, an illiquid or locked-up position, and a case in which conversion would work a substantial and uncompensated loss."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Duration and Release","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE VOLUNTARY ELECTION","body":"**(a)** An election continues for the duration of the electing judge's service.\n\n**(b)** A judge may be released from an election by the Conference upon a showing of hardship under \u00a7 2-2(f) that cannot be accommodated by an exception, or upon a change in the judge's circumstances that makes continued compliance unreasonable. A release is published in the database under Section 3, with the reason stated in general terms.\n\n**(c)** A release is prospective. It does not affect the judge's obligations during the period the election was in effect."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. No Effect on Compensation or Tenure","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE VOLUNTARY ELECTION","body":"Nothing in this Section or elsewhere in this Act diminishes the compensation of any judge, and no consequence of any kind attaches under this Act to the tenure of any judge. The obligations of an electing judge arise from the judge's own declaration and are enforced only as provided in Section 4."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. Establishment","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PUBLIC DATABASE","body":"The Administrative Office shall establish and maintain a public, searchable, machine-readable, bulk-downloadable database containing an entry for each covered judge, current and former.\n\n**(b) Publication does not wait on the database.** **A filing required by this Act is a public record from the moment it is filed, whether or not the database exists and whether or not it contains an entry.** Until the database contains an entry for a filing, the Administrative Office shall furnish a copy of the filing to any person on request, at no charge, within the period stated in the SCHEDULE. **The absence, incompleteness, or malfunction of the database is not a ground for withholding a filing** and does not suspend, toll, or diminish the ministerial duty under \u00a7 11-2D(b).","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PUBLIC DATABASE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. Contents","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PUBLIC DATABASE","body":"Each entry shall contain:\n\n**(a)** the judge's financial disclosure reports and transaction reports filed under Section 1, as published;\n\n**(b)** the court on which the judge sits and the dates of the judge's service;\n\n**(c)** whether the judge has made an election under Section 2, the date of any election, and the date and general reason for any release under \u00a7 2-3(b);\n\n**(d)** for an electing judge, the status of any conversion period under \u00a7 2-2(c) and of any hardship exception granted; and\n\n**(e)** the aggregate conflict-screening statistics published under \u00a7 1-3(d), presented for the judiciary as a whole and by court."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. Form and Parity","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PUBLIC DATABASE","body":"The database shall be maintained in a form permitting search and filter by name, court, and period of service, and shall be published in a structure comparable to the public databases of covered persons maintained under the Executive Integrity Act and the Congressional Integrity Act, following the common schema those Acts prescribe, so that the records of all three branches may be compared and merged by any person, so that the public may examine the judiciary's financial-integrity record on the same terms as that of other officeholders. The database is maintained by the judiciary.\n\nThe Administrative Office shall adopt the common schema published under Executive Integrity Act \u00a7 1-5(b-2), or shall publish the respects in which the judiciary's schema diverges and the reason for each divergence. Where that Act is not in effect, the Administrative Office publishes the schema it follows. No body directs another under this paragraph, and nothing in it subjects the judiciary to the direction of any officer outside it. Divergence is permitted; an unpublished divergence is not."},{"anchor":"sec-3-4","heading":"\u00a7 3-4. Neutral Presentation","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PUBLIC DATABASE","body":"The database shall present the fact of an election, and the fact that no election has been made, neutrally and without characterization. The absence of an election is not a finding, an allegation, or evidence of any impropriety, and shall not be presented as such. The purpose of publication is to inform the public, not to induce any judge to elect."},{"anchor":"sec-3-5","heading":"\u00a7 3-5. Judicial Security","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 THE PUBLIC DATABASE","body":"The Administrative Office shall redact from public publication any information whose disclosure would create a substantial risk to the personal safety of a judge or a judge's family, including a home address, and shall apply the protections of existing judicial-security law. A redaction shall be no broader than necessary and shall not conceal the identity, value, or category of any reportable asset."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Enforcement Within the Judiciary","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** This Act is administered and enforced exclusively by the Conference, by the judicial councils of the circuits, and by the Administrative Office. No officer or agency of the executive branch has authority to investigate, adjudicate, or impose any consequence under this Act.\n\n**(b)** A complaint that a covered judge has violated this Act is filed, considered, and resolved under the procedures of chapter 16 of title 28, United States Code, as those procedures apply to a complaint of conduct prejudicial to the effective and expeditious administration of the business of the courts.\n\n**(c)** The Conference shall prescribe rules implementing this Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. Screening of Complaints","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"A complaint under this Act shall not be published, and shall not be treated as a finding, until the reviewing authority has determined that it states probable cause to believe a violation occurred. A complaint failing that threshold is dismissed."},{"anchor":"sec-4-2a","heading":"\u00a7 4-2A. Advisory Opinions","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Authority.** The Judicial Conference, through a committee designated for the purpose, shall on written request issue a written opinion stating whether a described arrangement complies with this Act as to a judge of a court established under Article III other than the Supreme Court. The Administrative Office shall provide staff support to the committee for the preparation and publication of opinions; the committee's authority is decisional and the staff's role is ministerial.\n\n**(b) Effect.** An opinion is published and precedential. Reliance in good faith on an opinion is a complete defense to any consequence under this Act as to the arrangement described. An opinion does not survive a material omission or misrepresentation in the request.\n\n**(c) Deadline.** The Judicial Conference shall issue an opinion within 60 days of a complete request, and shall publish each opinion within 30 days of issuance.\n\n**(d) Supreme Court.** This Section does not reach the Supreme Court. A Justice of the Supreme Court may seek informal guidance from the Judicial Conference, which carries no binding effect and confers no defense; any mechanism for the Supreme Court is established, if at all, under \u00a7 8-1(c).\n\n**(e) De-identification and publication.** Every opinion issued under this Section shall be de-identified \u2014 removing the requester's name and any facts that would identify the requester \u2014 and published in a searchable, indexed library within 30 days of issuance. An opinion that cannot be de-identified without destroying its utility shall be published with the requester's consent or, absent consent, with a statement that a non-public opinion exists on the described subject.\n\n**(f) Shared advisory opinion library.** The Judicial Conference shall publish each opinion issued under this Section in a shared advisory opinion library maintained jointly with the bodies designated under the Executive Integrity Act and the Congressional Integrity Act. The library is an index and publication function; it is not a body, exercises no authority, and employs no staff beyond those of the contributing bodies.\n\n**(g) Applicability requests.** A covered judge may request a determination that a published opinion applies to the judge's arrangement. If the Judicial Conference determines that it does, the judge is entitled to the same reliance defense under subsection (b) as the original requester. If it does not, the Judicial Conference shall issue a new opinion, which is de-identified and published under subsection (e). An applicability request shall be decided within 30 days.\n\n**(h) Mutual recognition.** A covered judge may cite, in a request under this Section, an opinion issued by the enforcement body of another branch addressing the same or a substantially similar arrangement. Upon such a citation, the Judicial Conference shall, within 30 days: (1) **adopt** the cited opinion as applicable to this branch, in which case the requesting judge receives the same reliance defense as if the opinion had been issued under this Act, and the adoption is published; (2) **decline to adopt** the cited opinion without issuing a differing view, in which case the requesting judge may seek an opinion under subsection (a) and no inference arises from the declination; or (3) **issue a differing opinion**, which is published with a statement of the respects in which it differs and the reasons, creating a published split. An opinion adopted by all three branches is marked \"uniform\" in the shared library. A covered judge may rely on a uniform opinion without further request.\n\n**(i) Pending matters register.** The shared library shall include a register of pending matters. When the Judicial Conference receives a request addressing a novel question \u2014 one for which no opinion exists in the library \u2014 the Judicial Conference shall, within 7 days, post notice to the register identifying the subject matter in general terms. The notice shall not identify the requester, disclose confidential information, or describe the specific facts of the request.\n\n**(j) Independent action.** Each body acts independently. A body receiving a request on a subject posted to the register may wait for the pending opinion to issue and then adopt, diverge, or decline under subsection (h), or may proceed to issue its own opinion without waiting. No body is required to wait, to consult, or to conform its opinion to another's. No body shall share with another body the identity of a requester, the facts of a pending request, or any draft opinion. The register is a notice of subject matter only; it creates no joint proceeding, no shared deliberation, and no authority of one body over another.\n\n**(k) Tolling while pending.** Where a covered judge has submitted a request for an opinion and the Judicial Conference elects to hold that request pending the issuance of an opinion by another branch under subsection (j), any deadline under this Act that runs against the requester \u2014 including a deadline to divest, to elect, or to file \u2014 is tolled from the date the hold begins until the earlier of: (1) the date the opinion issues, or (2) the date the Judicial Conference elects to proceed independently. The requester shall be notified promptly when a hold begins and when it ends. No penalty, consequence, or adverse inference attaches to a requester by reason of a hold.\n\n**(l) Maximum hold.** A hold under subsection (k) shall not exceed 60 days. If the pending opinion has not issued within that period, the Judicial Conference shall proceed to issue an opinion or adopt, diverge, or decline based on any opinion then in the library.\n\n**(m) Adequate staffing.** The resources authorized under \u00a7 11-5 include staffing sufficient to issue opinions within the deadlines of this Section without routine resort to holds. The Administrative Office shall report annually the number of holds invoked, their average duration, and whether staffing is adequate to meet demand. A pattern of holds approaching the maximum under subsection (l) is evidence of inadequate staffing and shall be stated as such in the report.\n\n**(n) No super-agency.** Nothing in this Section creates a body with authority over more than one branch, compels any branch to adopt another's interpretation, or permits any branch to bind another. The Judicial Conference retains complete interpretive authority over covered judges within the judicial branch."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Cure of Good-Faith Error","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"A covered judge who, in good faith, files an incomplete or erroneous report and corrects it within 30 days after the error is known or reasonably should be known is not subject to any consequence under this Act for the error. The Administrative Office shall provide, without charge, standardized machine-readable filing tools designed to prevent common errors, and the availability of conforming tools is relevant to whether an error was made in good faith."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Remedies","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** The remedies available under this Act are those available under chapter 16 of title 28, together with an order directing compliance, an order directing recusal, an order directing divestiture of an asset held in violation of an election, an order directing the closure or divestiture of a position prohibited by \u00a7 6-10 together with disgorgement to the Treasury of any gain realized upon it, and publication of a finding.\n\n**(b)** No remedy under this Act includes removal from office, suspension of compensation, or any reduction of salary. Removal of a covered judge is governed by impeachment alone."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4a","heading":"\u00a7 4-4A. Investigative Authority","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** In a predicated investigation under this Act \u2014 meaning an investigation opened on a written determination, retained in the record, of specific and articulable facts giving reason to believe a violation occurred \u2014 the reviewing authority may issue a subpoena for financial records, testimony, and documents, and may apply to a district court for enforcement. The authority extends to a custodian, brokerage, bank, appraiser, counterparty, donor, entity, or other person holding records concerning the financial affairs of a covered judge or a family member, and the records of a covered judge held by such a person are reachable notwithstanding that the judge is not compelled to produce them.\n\n**(b)** A subpoena under this Section is issued by the judicial council of the circuit, or, as to a Justice, by the review panel under \u00a7 8-2, and is not issued by any officer of the executive branch.\n\n**(b-1) Capacity to conduct the investigation.** The reviewing authority may appoint counsel or a special master to conduct a predicated investigation and to issue subpoenas in the authority's name. A person so appointed shall not be an officer or employee of the executive branch. This subsection supplies the capacity the authority to issue presupposes; it confers no authority the reviewing authority does not have.\n\n**(c)** The reviewing authority shall provide the covered judge notice and an opportunity to respond before issuing a finding.\n\n**(d) Deadline.** The reviewing authority shall dispose of a complaint under this Act, by dismissal or by written finding, within 12 months of its filing, or shall publish a statement of the reason for the delay and the expected date of disposition, and shall renew the statement every 6 months thereafter. A complaint is not resolved by inaction.\n\n**(d-1) What a dismissal must state.** A dismissal of a predicated investigation shall state which of the specific and articulable facts in the written determination opening it were not substantiated. A dismissal that does not is not a disposition for purposes of subsection (d)."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4b","heading":"\u00a7 4-4B. Referral and Consequence","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Upon a sustained finding that a covered judge knowingly filed a materially false report or knowingly failed to file a required report, the reviewing authority shall refer the matter to the Attorney General for consideration under 5 U.S.C. \u00a7 13106 and any other applicable law, and shall publish the fact of the referral.\n\n**(b)** Referral under this Section is mandatory upon a sustained finding and is not discretionary.\n\n**(c)** The reviewing authority shall publish each referral and the disposition of each referral when known.\n\n**(d)** Nothing in this Section authorizes any executive officer to investigate a covered judge on any basis other than a referral under subsection (a) or an independently predicated criminal investigation."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. Annual Report","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"The Conference shall publish an annual report on the operation of this Act, including the number of electing judges, the number of releases granted, aggregate conflict-screening statistics, the number of complaints received, screened, and sustained, the remedies imposed, the number of referrals under \u00a7 4-4B, the disposition of complaints concerning Justices of the Supreme Court, and the number and disposition of matters before the review panel under \u00a7 8-2. The report shall not identify a judge in connection with a dismissed complaint."},{"anchor":"sec-4-6","heading":"\u00a7 4-6. Other Judicial Officers","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 ADMINISTRATION AND ENFORCEMENT","body":"The Conference shall apply Sections 1, 3, and 4 to other judicial officers, and shall make the election under Section 2 available to them, to the extent consistent with the statutes governing their offices."},{"anchor":"sec-5-0","heading":"\u00a7 5-0. Two Halves of This Section, Distinguished","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(b) Resting on established authority.** Section \u00a7 5-6, prescribing terms of active service in the courts of appeals and district courts and setting the number of judgeships on each, and \u00a7 5-A, providing a voluntary senior status for Justices of the Supreme Court, rest on Congress's established authority to ordain and establish the inferior courts, to fix the number of judges composing each court, and to prescribe the duties those judges perform. Neither presents an unsettled question. Nothing in either compels any Justice of the Supreme Court to cease the exercise of the judicial power.\n\n**(c) Offered as a model for a constitutional amendment; the question is unsettled.** Sections \u00a7 5-1 through \u00a7 5-5, prescribing terms of active service on the Supreme Court, are drafted so that they may serve as a model for an amendment under Article V. They are retained in this Act, and are severable under \u00a7 5-9, because a stated design is more useful to a public deliberation than a description of one.\n\n**(c-1) The question, stated fairly.** Whether Congress may prescribe a term of active service on the Supreme Court while preserving the office, the commission, the compensation, and removal by impeachment alone **has never been decided.** Congress has structured judicial duty since the First Congress, has changed the size of the Court seven times, and has controlled its terms and its quorum; senior status in the lower courts is long established. Against that, senior status in the lower courts is voluntary, and no Congress has removed a sitting Justice from participation in the Court's decisions while leaving the office intact. The difficulty most often identified is not tenure or compensation, neither of which these Sections disturb, but the Appointments Clause: a President nominates and the Senate confirms a person to an office, and it is contested whether the office and the exercise of its power are separable in the manner these Sections assume.\n\n**(c-2) How this Act treats the question.** These Sections are enacted, if enacted, on the understanding that the question is open and that the weight of opinion is unfavorable. The Act does not rest on them. Section \u00a7 5-A supplies a voluntary structure that operates whether or not they are sustained, \u00a7 5-6 rests on separate authority, and \u00a7 5-9 quarantines these Sections from every other provision of this Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"*This Section is wholly severable from the remainder of this Act, and each of its provisions is severable from every other, as provided in \u00a7 5-9.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-a","heading":"\u00a7 5-A. Voluntary Senior Status for Justices of the Supreme Court","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a) Ordinary term of active service.** The ordinary term of active service on the Supreme Court of the United States is 18 years. This subsection states an expectation and compels nothing.\n\n**(b) Election.** A Justice may elect senior status at any time, by filing a written declaration with the Administrative Office. The election is voluntary in every case and at every time.\n\n**(c) Statement of intention to continue.** A Justice who remains in active service after completing 18 years shall file annually a written statement of intention to continue in active service, which the Administrative Office shall publish. The statement requires no reason and no explanation, and no consequence of any kind attaches to it or to the decision it records.\n\n**(d) Publication of service.** The Administrative Office shall publish the date of commission and the length of active service of each Justice, and the Judicial Conference shall report annually on the composition of the Court.\n\n**(e) Senior status is a continuation of judicial service.** A Senior Justice continues to hold the office of Justice of the Supreme Court during good behavior, retains the commission, the title, and the compensation of a Justice in active service, which shall not be diminished, and is removable only by impeachment. Senior status under this Section is not a retirement, and this Act shall not be construed to treat it as one.\n\n**(f) A judicial docket.** A Senior Justice is entitled, upon request, to assignment to sit on any court of appeals of the United States, and the Chief Justice shall make the assignment. The Administrative Office shall provide chambers, law clerks, and staff on the scale provided to a judge in regular active service on that court.\n\n**(g) Recall to the Supreme Court.** A Senior Justice sits in active service on the Supreme Court in each matter in which the participating Justices in active service are fewer than a quorum, until its final disposition, or in place of a Justice in active service who is recused in a matter. A Senior Justice recalled under this subsection exercises the full judicial power in the matter, and sits without designation or assignment; section 294 of title 28, United States Code, does not apply to the sitting. Section 2109 of title 28, United States Code, applies to a matter only if the participating Justices remain fewer than a quorum after this subsection operates. A Senior Justice so sitting counts toward the quorum in that matter. The Clerk of the Supreme Court shall enter on the docket of each matter in which this subsection applies the name of each Senior Justice who sits, or that none sat. **No Senior Justice sits in place of a seat that is empty under \u00a7 5-5(b).**\n\n**(h) Role in the administration of the judiciary.** A Senior Justice may be designated by the Chief Justice to serve on the Judicial Conference of the United States, on any committee established under the Rules Enabling Act, on the Board of the Federal Judicial Center, and in any other capacity in the administration of the courts of the United States. The Chief Justice shall designate not fewer than one Senior Justice to the Judicial Conference and to each rules committee, if any Senior Justice is willing to serve.\n\n**(i) Membership in the institution.** A Senior Justice retains precedence, membership, and participation in the ceremonial sessions of the Supreme Court, the investiture of a Justice, the Court's conferences to the extent the Court provides, and the life of the institution.\n\n**(j) External ceremonies.** This Act makes no provision concerning the attendance of any Justice, in active or senior status, at the inauguration of a President, at a joint session of Congress, or at any other occasion outside the judiciary. Attendance at such occasions is governed by custom and by the choice of the individual Justice, as it is now, and nothing in this Act creates an expectation, an entitlement, or a bar.\n\n**(k) The second half of a career.** A Justice completing 18 years of active service will commonly be in the seventh decade of life and will not have finished working. This Section is drafted to describe a different judicial portfolio rather than the end of one, and its provisions are to be construed to that end.\n\n**(l) No compensation inducement.** Nothing in this Section provides compensation, allowance, or emolument to a Senior Justice beyond that of a Justice in active service.\n\n**(m) Effect independent of the remainder of this Section.** This Section operates whether or not \u00a7\u00a7 5-1 through 5-5 are enacted, sustained, or ever take effect, and is severable from them under \u00a7 5-9."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. Term of Active Service on the Supreme Court","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a)** A Justice of the Supreme Court of the United States shall serve in active service on that Court for a term of 18 years.\n\n**(b)** Upon the conclusion of the term, the Justice assumes the status of Senior Justice under \u00a7 5-2 and ceases to sit in active service on the Supreme Court, except as provided in \u00a7 5-A(g).\n\n**(c)** This Section does not remove any Justice from office, terminate any commission, or reduce any compensation. It prescribes the duties to be performed by a judge of the United States who holds a commission to the Supreme Court, which is a matter of the organization of the courts."},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Senior Justice","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a) Office retained.** A Senior Justice continues to hold the office of Justice of the Supreme Court of the United States during good behavior, retains the commission and the title, and receives the compensation of a Justice in active service, which shall not be diminished.\n\n**(b) Duties.** A Senior Justice may sit by designation on any court of appeals or district court of the United States, and shall perform such judicial duties as the Senior Justice consents to undertake, in the manner of a judge who has retired from regular active service under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 371(b).\n\n**(c) Recall.** A Senior Justice sits in active service on the Supreme Court when recalled under \u00a7 5-A(g).\n\n**(d) Removal.** A Senior Justice may be removed from office only by impeachment."},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Schedule of Openings","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a)** One seat in active service on the Supreme Court shall become open on July 1 of each odd-numbered year.\n\n**(c)** A seat that becomes open is filled by appointment in the manner provided by Article II, Section 2. A Justice so appointed serves in active service until July 1 of the eighteenth year following, whereupon the seat again becomes open. Nothing in this Section directs the President in the exercise of the nomination power or the Senate in the exercise of advice and consent."},{"anchor":"sec-5-4","heading":"\u00a7 5-4. Transition for Justices in Service at Enactment","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a)** The seats opening under \u00a7 5-3 shall be filled in the first instance by the rotation of the Justices in active service on the date of enactment into Senior Justice status, in descending order of length of continuous service on the Supreme Court, the longest-serving Justice rotating at the first opening, the next longest-serving at the second, and so on.\n\n**(b)** The first opening under this Section occurs on July 1 of the first odd-numbered year beginning at least one year after the date of enactment.\n\n**(c)** A Justice who rotates into Senior Justice status under this Section does so without regard to whether the Justice has served 18 years, and the rotation is complete when each Justice in active service on the date of enactment has rotated.\n\n**(d)** This Section identifies Justices by length of service and not by name, and applies to whichever Justices are in active service on the date of enactment."},{"anchor":"sec-5-5","heading":"\u00a7 5-5. Death, Incapacity, and Interim Vacancy","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a) Successor serves the remainder of the term.** Where a seat becomes vacant before the conclusion of its term by reason of death, resignation, retirement, removal, or a determination of incapacity, the Justice appointed to that seat serves only until the date on which the term of that seat would otherwise have concluded. The schedule of openings under \u00a7 5-3 is not altered by an interim vacancy.\n\n**(b) Open seats.** Where a seat in active service is vacant, whether by an interim vacancy or because no appointment has been made to a seat that has become open under \u00a7 5-3, the seat stays empty until it is filled. No Justice continues in active service in that seat, and no Senior Justice sits in it, by reason of the vacancy.\n\n**(c) Purpose.** Subsection (b) ensures that a failure to appoint keeps no Justice in active service on the Supreme Court past the conclusion of a term. While a seat is empty, the Court consists of the Justices then in active service, and its quorum is as provided by subsection (e).\n\n**(d) Incapacity.** A determination of incapacity of a Justice for purposes of this Section is made by the Supreme Court under procedures it prescribes, the Justice in question not participating. A determination of incapacity of a judge of a lower court is made by the judicial council of the circuit under chapter 16 of title 28. No such determination affects the tenure, office, or compensation of the judge or Justice.\n\n**(e) Quorum.** Section 1 of title 28, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **\u00a7 1. Number of justices; quorum**\n>\n> The Supreme Court of the United States shall consist of a Chief Justice of the United States and eight associate justices, ~~any six of whom shall constitute a quorum~~ **and not fewer than two-thirds of the justices then in regular active service on the Court shall constitute a quorum. A Senior Justice, and a justice who has retired from regular active service, is not counted among the justices in regular active service for purposes of this section, but a Senior Justice sitting in a matter under section 5-A(g) of the Judiciary Integrity Act counts toward the quorum in that matter**."},{"anchor":"sec-5-6","heading":"\u00a7 5-6. Terms of Active Service in the Lower Courts","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a)** A judge of a United States court of appeals, a United States district court, or the Court of International Trade shall serve in active service on that court for a term of 18 years, and upon its conclusion shall assume senior status under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 371(b), retaining office, commission, title, and undiminished compensation.\n\n**(b) Composition set to a divisor of 216.** A term of 18 years is 216 months. So that openings on every court fall at equal intervals measured in whole months, the number of authorized judgeships on each United States court of appeals, each United States district court, and the Court of International Trade is set at the least divisor of 216 that is not less than the number authorized on the date of enactment. The divisors of 216 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 12, 18, 24, 27, 36, 54, 72, 108, and 216. A court whose authorized number is already a divisor of 216 is unchanged.\n\n**(b-1) Additional judgeships.** There are authorized such additional judgeships on each court as are necessary to reach the number set by subsection (b). An additional judgeship so authorized is a judgeship of that court in every respect and is filled by appointment in the manner provided by Article II, Section 2.\n\n**(b-2) Staggering.** The seats in active service on each court shall be staggered so that an opening occurs at intervals of 216 months divided by the number of authorized judgeships on that court.\n\n**(b-3) Published calendar.** The Judicial Conference shall publish, for each court, a fixed calendar of opening dates at the interval required by subsection (b-2), and shall reset the calendar upon any change in the number of authorized judgeships. Where Congress changes that number, it is the policy of this Section that the number be set at a divisor of 216 so that equal whole-month intervals are preserved; where the number is not a divisor of 216, the Conference shall publish a calendar at intervals as nearly equal as the number permits. A term already running is not shortened by a reset.\n\n**(c) Transition and phase-in of additional judgeships.** On each court, the seats shall be brought onto the calendar published under subsection (b-3) beginning on the first day of the first calendar quarter beginning at least one year after enactment. At each successive opening date, one seat is filled, taken in the following order: first, the seat of the judge in active service having the longest continuous active service on that court, who thereupon assumes senior status in the manner of \u00a7 5-4; then an additional judgeship authorized under subsection (b-1); and so alternating until every sitting judge has been brought onto the calendar and every additional judgeship has been filled. The Judicial Conference shall publish the order for each court in advance.\n\n**(c-1) Purpose of the phase-in.** Subsection (c) interleaves the rotation of sitting judges with the filling of additional judgeships, and spreads both across the full calendar, so that no single President or Senate appoints the additional judgeships of a court at one time, and so that the composition of each court changes gradually.\n\n**(d) Existing senior status preserved.** Nothing in this Section reduces the eligibility of any judge to assume senior status earlier under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 371, or alters the terms on which senior status is held.\n\n**(e) Open seats.** A seat in active service that is vacant stays vacant until it is filled, and no judge continues in active service in that seat by reason of the vacancy. A judge who has assumed senior status under subsection (a) performs judicial duties as provided in 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 294 and sits on a court in banc only as 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 46(c) provides.\n\n**(f) Interim vacancy.** A judge appointed to a seat vacated before the conclusion of its term serves only until the date on which that term would otherwise have concluded."},{"anchor":"sec-5-7","heading":"\u00a7 5-7. Compensation and Tenure Preserved","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a)** No provision of this Section diminishes the compensation of any judge or Justice. A judge or Justice who ceases active service under this Section continues to receive the compensation of a judge or Justice in active service on the same court, without reduction, for life.\n\n**(b)** No provision of this Section removes any judge or Justice from office, shortens any commission, or affects tenure during good behavior. Removal remains available only by impeachment.\n\n**(c)** If any application of this Section would diminish the compensation of a judge or Justice, or would operate as a removal from office, that application is void and the remainder of this Section continues in force."},{"anchor":"sec-5-8","heading":"\u00a7 5-8. Constitutional Basis","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"Congress enacts this Section pursuant to Article I, Section 8, Clause 9, Article III, Section 1, and Article I, Section 8, Clause 18, under which Congress ordains and establishes the courts inferior to the Supreme Court, fixes the number of judges composing each court, and prescribes the duties those judges perform. This Section allocates judicial duty among judges who hold office during good behavior. It does not create or abolish any judicial office, does not shorten any tenure, and does not diminish any compensation."},{"anchor":"sec-5-9","heading":"\u00a7 5-9. Severability of This Section","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 TERMS OF ACTIVE SERVICE","body":"**(a) From the Act.** This Section is severable in its entirety from the remainder of this Act. If any provision of this Section is held invalid, Sections 0 through 4 and Sections 6 through 11 remain in full force, and the invalidity of this Section shall not be construed to affect any other provision of this Act.\n\n**(a-1) No aggregation.** This Section is to be applied together with \u00a7 11-2A. A holding that any provision of this Section is invalid, including a holding that Congress may not prescribe terms of active service on the Supreme Court, shall not be extended to any other Section of this Act on the ground that the provisions together constitute a scheme of legislative supervision of the judiciary. Congress would have enacted every other Section of this Act in the absence of this Section, and directs that each be preserved accordingly.\n\n**(a-2) Purpose preserved on an adverse holding.** If \u00a7\u00a7 5-1 through 5-5 are held to be beyond the power of Congress, or to require an amendment to the Constitution, that holding does not disturb the remainder of this Act, and the provisions so held may serve as a model for such an amendment. The retention of these Sections is not a representation that the question is settled in either direction, and \u00a7 5-0(c-1) states the competing considerations.\n\n**(b) Supreme Court from lower courts.** The application of this Section to the Supreme Court under \u00a7\u00a7 5-1 through 5-5 is severable from its application to the lower courts under \u00a7 5-6. If the application to the Supreme Court is held invalid, \u00a7 5-6 remains in force, and the reverse.\n\n**(b-1) The voluntary provision stands alone.** Section \u00a7 5-A, providing a voluntary senior status for Justices of the Supreme Court, is severable in its entirety from \u00a7\u00a7 5-1 through 5-5 and from \u00a7 5-6, operates whether or not either is enacted or sustained, and compels nothing of any Justice. A holding that \u00a7\u00a7 5-1 through 5-5 exceed the power of Congress does not reach \u00a7 5-A, which imposes no term and prescribes no consequence for remaining in active service.\n\n**(b-2) Within \u00a7 5-A.** Each subsection of \u00a7 5-A is severable from every other. If the statement of intention required by \u00a7 5-A(c) is held invalid, the entitlements of subsections (f) through (i) continue; and if any entitlement is held invalid, the expectation and the publication requirements continue.\n\n**(c) Sitting from future.** The application of this Section to a judge or Justice in active service on the date of enactment is severable from its application to a judge or Justice appointed thereafter. If the application to those in service at enactment is held invalid, this Section applies to each judge and Justice appointed after enactment, and the schedule under \u00a7 5-3 and \u00a7 5-6(b) commences with the first appointment made after enactment.\n\n**(d) Open seats and quorum.** The open-seat provisions of \u00a7 5-5(b) and \u00a7 5-6(e), and the quorum provision of \u00a7 5-5(e), are severable from the term provisions, and a holding that a term provision is invalid does not affect them.\n\n**(e) Each seat.** The application of this Section to each seat is severable from its application to every other seat."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. Reporting of Gifts","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"**(a)** A covered judge shall report each gift, and each item of transportation, lodging, food, or entertainment, received by the judge or by a family member, from any source other than a relative, valued above a threshold set in the schedule to this Act, identifying the donor, the date, the nature of the item, and its value.\n\n**(b)** Gifts from a single source shall be aggregated over the reporting year for purposes of the threshold.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. The Personal Hospitality Exemption, Narrowed","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"**(a)** The exemption from reporting for personal hospitality does not apply to:\n\n1. transportation that substitutes for commercial transportation, including travel by private aircraft, chartered aircraft, or private or chartered vessel;\n\n2. lodging at a commercial property, a resort, a hotel, a club, a hunting or fishing lodge, or a property operated as a commercial enterprise, whether or not a charge is made;\n\n3. hospitality extended by a corporation, partnership, association, or other entity, as distinct from a natural person;\n\n4. hospitality extended by a person who has, or whose entity has, a matter pending before the judge's court, or who has appeared as a party, counsel, or amicus in the judge's court within the preceding three years; or\n\n5. hospitality extended by a person who has contributed to an organization that has appeared as amicus in the judge's court, where the judge knows of the contribution; or\n\n6. hospitality extended by a person who has, within the preceding three years, contributed above the threshold in \u00a7 10-1(a) to an organization whose regular activity includes litigation in the courts of the United States, where the judge knows of the contribution.\n\n**(a-1) Revocable trusts controlled by a natural person.** For purposes of subsection (a)(3), hospitality extended through a trust that is revocable by, and wholly controlled by, a single natural person or by natural persons who are relatives of one another, is treated as extended by that natural person or those persons, and not by an entity. This subsection does not extend to an irrevocable trust, a trust with a corporate or independent trustee exercising discretion, or an entity with any member, partner, or beneficial owner other than the natural persons described in this subsection.\n\n**(b)** This Section codifies and extends the narrowing of the personal-hospitality exemption adopted by the Judicial Conference in March 2023, and applies it to every covered judge, including a Justice of the Supreme Court."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Valuation of In-Kind Benefits","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"**(a)** An item reportable under this Section is valued at the fair market value a member of the public would pay to obtain it, and not at the cost to the donor or at zero. Travel by private or chartered aircraft or vessel is valued at the prevailing charter rate for the aircraft or vessel and itinerary, determined by reference to a published commercial charter index identified by the Judicial Conference. Lodging is valued at the prevailing published rate for comparable accommodation.\n\n**(a-1) Where no published rate exists.** Where no published rate covers the item \u2014 including travel on an aircraft or vessel that is privately owned rather than chartered, and the use of a privately owned residence \u2014 the item is valued at the greater of the donor's cost of providing it and the value produced by a formula the Judicial Conference shall prescribe under subsection (b). The absence of a market price is not a ground for reporting no value.\n\n**(b) Binding rules, subordinate to the standard.** The Judicial Conference shall prescribe valuation rules under this Section by rule. The rules are binding and are not advisory guidance. **A rule under this subsection implements subsections (a) and (a-1) and may not value an item below the standard those subsections state.** **The absence of a rule does not suspend subsection (a) or (a-1)**, which state the operative standard and apply of their own force.\n\n**(c) Use of property.** The use of real or personal property without charge, or at a charge below the rate a member of the public would pay, is an item reportable under this Section, valued at the difference. This subsection reaches the use of a residence, a vehicle, a vessel, an aircraft, and a club or resort membership.\n\n**(d) Look-through.** An item provided through a trust, corporation, partnership, limited liability company, or other entity is attributed to the natural persons who control or fund the entity, and is reportable as an item from those persons.\n\n**(e) The reference sources are published.** The Judicial Conference shall publish the charter index it identifies under subsection (a), the rates applied under it, and any formula prescribed under subsection (a-1), and shall republish upon any change. A source not published may not be relied upon to value an item under this Section.\n\n**(f) A valuation below the published rate states its basis.** A valuation reported below the rate produced by the published source shall state the basis for the difference. A valuation stating no basis does not satisfy this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-6-4","heading":"\u00a7 6-4. Real Property Transactions","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"**(a)** A covered judge shall report each purchase, sale, exchange, or transfer of an interest in real property by the judge or a family member, identifying the counterparty, the consideration, and the date, regardless of value.\n\n**(b)** Where the counterparty is not dealing at arm's length, or where the consideration differs materially from fair market value, the judge shall so state and shall report the difference. A transfer at other than fair market value is a gift to the extent of the difference and is reportable under \u00a7 6-1.\n\n**(c)** This Section reaches a transaction in which the counterparty is an entity, and requires identification of the natural persons controlling that entity.\n\n**(d)** This Section reaches a transaction in which the judge's interest is held through a trust, corporation, partnership, limited liability company, or other entity, and a transaction by such an entity is reportable as a transaction by the judge to the extent of the judge's interest."},{"anchor":"sec-6-5","heading":"\u00a7 6-5. Debt Forgiven, Restructured, or Extended on Favorable Terms","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"A covered judge shall report each extension of credit to the judge or a family member on terms not available to a similarly situated borrower, and each forgiveness, cancellation, restructuring, or non-enforcement of a debt. The amount forgiven or the value of the favorable terms is reportable as a gift under \u00a7 6-1 and is income to the extent provided by law."},{"anchor":"sec-6-6","heading":"\u00a7 6-6. Outside Income, Advances, and Teaching","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"A covered judge shall report each item of outside income, including a royalty, an advance against royalties, a fee for teaching, and a payment for an article or other work, identifying the payer and the amount. Where a payer has a matter pending before the judge's court, or has appeared in the judge's court within the preceding three years, the judge shall so state."},{"anchor":"sec-6-7","heading":"\u00a7 6-7. Appearances and Sponsored Events","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"A covered judge shall report each appearance at an event for which travel, lodging, or a fee was provided, or which was organized as a fundraising or donor event, identifying the organizer, each entity providing financial support for the event that is known to the judge, and any funder that has appeared as a party, counsel, or amicus in the judge's court within the preceding three years."},{"anchor":"sec-6-8","heading":"\u00a7 6-8. Coordination With Federal Tax Reporting","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"**(a)** The Administrative Office shall transmit to the Secretary of the Treasury a report of each gift reported under this Section whose value exceeds the annual exclusion amount under 26 U.S.C. \u00a7 2503(b), for the purpose of the administration of the gift tax.\n\n**(b)** For each gift reported whose value exceeds the annual exclusion amount, the covered judge shall certify whether a Federal gift-tax return was filed with respect to the gift and, if the judge is aware that none was filed, shall so state. The certification is transmitted with the report under subsection (a) and is published.\n\n**(c)** Reporting under this Section does not determine the tax treatment of any item, and nothing in this Section imposes a tax."},{"anchor":"sec-6-9","heading":"\u00a7 6-9. Gifts From Persons With Matters Before the Court","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"**(a)** A covered judge shall not accept a gift, or transportation, lodging, food, or entertainment, from a person who has a matter pending before the judge, who has appeared before the judge as a party, counsel, or amicus within the preceding three years, or who has given the judge or the court written notice of an intention to file a matter.\n\n**(b)** Where a judge has accepted an item from a person who thereafter appears before the judge, the judge shall disclose the item on the record in that matter and shall consider it under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455.\n\n**(c)** This Section does not reach an item from a relative, an item of nominal value, or an item that would be permitted under the rules governing gifts to officers and employees of the United States generally."},{"anchor":"sec-6-10","heading":"\u00a7 6-10. Prediction-Market Positions and Event Wagers","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** A covered individual shall not enter into, acquire, hold, or maintain a covered event position, whether directly or through any structure, arrangement, entity, trust, nominee, or intermediary, and regardless of form, title, or characterization. The prohibition of this subsection does not depend on the covered individual holding any non-public information, and applies whatever the event on which the position turns.\n\n**(b) Covered individual defined.** For purposes of this Section, \"covered individual\" means:\n\n1. each covered judge, each other judicial officer, and each officer and employee of the judicial branch of the United States, including an officer or employee of the Administrative Office, the Federal Judicial Center, and the United States Sentencing Commission; and\n\n2. each individual who has access to non-public information of the United States in the performance of an arrangement with a court of the United States or with the Administrative Office, the Federal Judicial Center, the United States Sentencing Commission, or another office or instrumentality of the judicial branch, within the meaning of subsection (b-1), during the period of that access.\n\n**(b-1) Access to non-public information under an arrangement.** For purposes of subsection (b)(2), of \u00a7 6-11, and of a certification under subsection (h):\n\n1. \"Arrangement with the United States\" means a contract, a subcontract at any tier, a grant, a cooperative agreement, an other-transaction agreement, an intergovernmental or interagency agreement, a detail, a fellowship, or any other arrangement, however denominated, under which an individual performs services or other work for, on behalf of, or with funds provided by the United States or an agency, office, court, or instrumentality of the United States, whether the individual performs them as a party to the arrangement or as an owner, officer, employee, member, partner, or agent of a party. The forms enumerated in this paragraph are illustrative, and an arrangement of any other form under which services are so performed is an arrangement with the United States regardless of its title or characterization.\n\n2. \"Non-public information of the United States\" means information held by the United States or by an agency, office, court, or instrumentality of the United States that has not been made generally available to the public, and includes classified information, information the disclosure of which a statute or regulation restricts, information held in a non-public information system, information concerning a matter pending before a court that is not on the public record, and information concerning a pending or contemplated action, decision, procurement, investigation, adjudication, or proceeding.\n\n3. An individual has access to non-public information of the United States during any period in which such information is made available to the individual in the performance of an arrangement with the United States. An individual has such access, without regard to any other fact, during any period in which the individual holds, by reason of the arrangement, a security clearance issued by the United States or an account on a non-public information system of the United States.\n\n4. An individual whose performance of an arrangement consists only of the supply of goods, or of services or other work performed without access to non-public information of the United States, is not a covered individual under subsection (b)(2). A party to an arrangement is not a covered individual by reason of an individual's access, and this Section imposes no duty on a party to an arrangement other than the duties stated in subsection (i). The exclusions of this paragraph are those and only those stated in it.\n\n5. Subsection (b)(2) applies to an individual without regard to the individual's nationality or to the place at which the arrangement is performed, and this Section applies to a covered event position entered into by such an individual wherever the individual is located. The application of this Section to an individual described in subsection (b)(2) is severable from its application to every other covered individual, and the application of \u00a7 6-11 to such an individual is severable from every other application of that Section.\n\n**(c) Covered event position defined.** For purposes of this Section, \"covered event position\" means an event contract, a prediction-market position, a binary option on the occurrence of an event, a wager, and any other contract, position, or arrangement the value or payout of which depends upon the occurrence, non-occurrence, timing, or outcome of an event, entered into on or through a market, exchange, platform, or other venue that offers such positions to the public or to any class of persons, whether access is open or conditioned upon membership, invitation, registration, or qualification \u2014 whether or not the position is regulated as a security or commodity interest, and whether or not the venue is organized or operated within the United States.\n\n**(d) What is not a covered event position.** A covered event position does not include:\n\n1. an instrument the value of which depends upon the price or level of a security, commodity, currency, rate of interest, or published index of prices or values, and not upon the occurrence, non-occurrence, timing, or outcome of a discrete specified event;\n\n2. a contract of insurance, an annuity, or a warranty or guarantee incidental to a sale of goods or services;\n\n3. a wager on an athletic contest or a game of chance, placed under a license issued under the law of a State or of an Indian tribe, or with a lottery conducted under the law of a State; or\n\n4. a position entered into in the ordinary course of a bona fide trade or business, to hedge or manage a commercial risk arising from the operations of that business, and not for speculation; this paragraph does not extend to a position on an election, on the action or inaction of a government or an officer of a government, or on litigation.\n\nThe exclusions of this subsection are those and only those stated in it, and the omission of any other exclusion is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(e) Closure.** A covered event position held on the date this Section takes effect, or held on the date a person first becomes a covered individual, shall be closed or divested within 90 days of that date. A position arising without the covered individual's action is not a violation until the individual knows or reasonably should know of it, and shall be closed or divested within 90 days of that knowledge.\n\n**(f) A position held or placed by another.** A covered event position entered into by another person is the covered individual's for purposes of this Section where the covered individual directed, requested, or arranged it, furnished the funds staked upon it, or is entitled to share in its proceeds. A covered event position held by a family member of a covered judge is reportable as Section 1 provides, and bears on the judge's obligation under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455 where its value depends on a matter before the judge; this Section does not otherwise compel a family member.\n\n**(g) Void.** A covered event position entered into in violation of this Section is void, and no venue or counterparty acquires any right upon it against any person.\n\n**(h) Certification.** A covered individual shall state the fact of the individual's service or access truthfully in each certification concerning service as an officer or employee of the United States, or access to non-public information of the United States under an arrangement with the United States, that a venue requests in connection with an account or a position, and a false statement in such a certification is a violation of this Section.\n\n**(i) Enforcement within the judiciary.** As to a covered judge, this Section is enforced as Section 4 provides, and the remedies include an order directing closure or divestiture and an order directing disgorgement to the Treasury of any gain realized, as \u00a7 4-4(a) provides. As to a covered individual who is not a covered judge, a violation of this Section is a basis for disciplinary action under the authority governing the individual's employment, including removal from employment, and, as to an individual described in subsection (b)(2), for removal of the individual from performance of the arrangement and from the access it affords. The prohibition of subsection (a) is a term of every arrangement under which an individual described in subsection (b)(2) has access, whether or not the arrangement states it, and neither the omission of the term nor any provision of the arrangement affects its application. Each party to such an arrangement shall give each such individual written notice of this Section before the individual's access begins or, for an individual having access on the date this Section takes effect, within 30 days of that date, and shall retain a record of the notice; a notice that does not identify this Section and state the prohibition of subsection (a) and the closure period of subsection (e) does not satisfy this sentence. A failure to give notice is a breach of the arrangement by the party and excuses no individual, and an individual's violation is not a breach of the arrangement by the party. No officer or agency of the executive branch acquires any authority under this Section. An amount disgorged under this Section is not deductible for any tax purpose, and no such amount is treated as restitution, remediation, or an amount paid to come into compliance with law for purposes of section 162(f)(2) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986.\n\n**(j) Relation to other law.** This Section is in addition to, and not in lieu of, every other prohibition or obligation, including 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455 and the disclosure obligations of this Act, and nothing in this Section authorizes a position that any other provision prohibits."},{"anchor":"sec-6-11","heading":"\u00a7 6-11. Communication of Non-Public Information","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 GIFTS, TRAVEL, HOSPITALITY, OUTSIDE INCOME, WAGERS, AND NON-PUBLIC INFORMATION","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** A covered individual within the meaning of \u00a7 6-10(b) shall not communicate material non-public information obtained in the course of official duties, by reason of the position held, or in the performance of an arrangement with the United States or by reason of the access it affords, to any person, where the covered individual knows or has reason to believe that the person may acquire, hold, or dispose of a financial instrument on the basis of it, unless the communication is an authorized disclosure.\n\n**(b) Definitions.** For purposes of this Section, \"financial instrument\" includes a security, commodity interest, futures or forward contract, option, swap, other derivative, event contract, prediction-market position, wager, and any other contract, instrument, or arrangement the value of which depends upon the occurrence or non-occurrence of an event; and \"authorized disclosure\" means a communication made in the proper discharge of official duties, including a deliberation, opinion, order, or docket entry of a court; a dissemination that makes the information generally available to the public; a disclosure required by law or made in a judicial, legislative, or administrative proceeding; a disclosure to the Congress, to an Inspector General, or to a law-enforcement authority; and a disclosure protected by a whistleblower statute. The enumeration of authorized disclosures is exclusive, and the omission of any other class of disclosure is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(c) Enforcement within the judiciary.** As to a covered judge, this Section is enforced as Section 4 provides. As to a covered individual who is not a covered judge, a violation of this Section is a basis for disciplinary action under the authority governing the individual's employment, including removal from employment, and, as to an individual described in \u00a7 6-10(b)(2), for removal of the individual from performance of the arrangement and from the access it affords. No officer or agency of the executive branch acquires any authority under this Act by reason of this Section, and nothing in this Section limits the operation of any generally applicable law outside this Act.\n\n**(d) Relation to other law.** This Section is in addition to, and not in lieu of, 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455, the Code of Conduct as applied by \u00a7 8-1, \u00a7 6-10, and every other prohibition or obligation, and nothing in this Section authorizes a communication that any other provision prohibits."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Statement of Reasons for Disqualification","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 RECUSAL AND CASE ASSIGNMENT","body":"**(a)** A covered judge who is disqualified from a matter under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455 shall enter on the docket a brief statement identifying the ground of disqualification by reference to the subsection relied upon. The statement need not disclose the identity of an asset or the details of a relationship where identification would be inappropriate, and may state the ground in general terms.\n\n**(b)** A covered judge who denies a motion for disqualification shall state the reasons in writing.\n\n**(c)** This Section does not create a ground for disqualification, alter the standard, or make any disqualification decision reviewable that is not otherwise reviewable.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 7 \u2014 RECUSAL AND CASE ASSIGNMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Supreme Court Disqualification Procedure","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 RECUSAL AND CASE ASSIGNMENT","body":"**(a) Rule required.** The Supreme Court shall adopt, publish, and follow a written procedure governing the disposition of a motion for the disqualification of a Justice.\n\n**(b) Subjects addressed.** The procedure shall address: how a motion is presented; who determines it; whether and how the determination is stated; the time within which it is determined; and whether the determination is entered on the docket. Congress prescribes the subjects a procedure must address and does not prescribe how the Court resolves any of them.\n\n**(c) Application on the record.** Each disposition of a motion for disqualification shall be entered on the docket, identifying the procedure applied. **Where no procedure adopted under subsection (a) has been published, the entry shall state that fact.** **This subsection operates whether or not subsection (a) has been complied with, and the absence of a procedure is recorded rather than presumed.**\n\n**(d) Reservation.** Nothing in this Section requires a Justice to participate in or withdraw from any matter, prescribes the content of any procedure, subjects any determination to review, or authorizes any person to compel a particular disposition.\n\n**(e) Existing rules suffice.** A procedure already adopted and published that addresses the subjects in subsection (b) satisfies this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Random Assignment in Cases Seeking Broad Relief","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 RECUSAL AND CASE ASSIGNMENT","body":"**(a)** In a civil action in a district court in which a party seeks to restrain or invalidate the enforcement of a Federal statute, regulation, or executive action, or a State statute or regulation, on a basis that would afford relief beyond the parties or beyond the district, the case shall be assigned by random selection from among all judges in active service in the district, without regard to the division in which the case is filed.\n\n**(a-1) The trigger cannot be waited out.** Where a party first seeks relief of the scope described in subsection (a) after the case has been assigned \u2014 by amendment, by motion, or otherwise \u2014 the case shall be reassigned under subsection (a). Assignment under a narrower pleading confers nothing that survives the broadening of the relief sought.\n\n**(a-2) A pool of at least three.** Where, at the time a case is assigned or reassigned under this Section, fewer than three judges in active service in the district are not disqualified from it, the selection under subsection (a) is made from those judges together with each senior judge of the district not disqualified from it who is listed, on the date the case was filed, on the roster kept under this subsection; and if the pool is still fewer than three, district judges in active service elsewhere in the circuit and not disqualified from the case, drawn by lot in public by the clerk of the court of appeals, are added until it is three. A senior judge who undertakes in writing to hear any case assigned under this Section files the undertaking with the clerk of the district court, who shall enter it on a roster the clerk publishes. An undertaking takes effect on the first day of the calendar year after it is filed and continues until withdrawn by a filing that takes effect on the same terms, and the clerk shall publish the date of each undertaking and of each withdrawal. The clerk of the district court shall enter the pool and each draw on the docket. A senior judge so selected shall be designated and assigned to the case by the chief judge of the circuit under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 294(c), the undertaking being the judge's consent, and an undertaking may not be withdrawn as to a case already drawn; and a judge from elsewhere in the circuit so selected shall be designated and assigned to it by the chief judge of the circuit under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 292(b), the designation being in the public interest for purposes of that section. A designation or assignment under this subsection lasts until the final disposition of the case and, notwithstanding the last sentence of 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 295, may be revoked only upon the judge's disqualification, death, ceasing to hold office, or physical or mental incapacity to perform the duties of the case; on revocation the case is reassigned under this Section as on its first assignment.\n\n**(b)** Subsection (a) applies notwithstanding any local rule or standing order of the district providing for assignment within a division.\n\n**(c)** The Judicial Conference shall prescribe the method of random selection. **Until it does, and in any district for which no method has been prescribed, assignment under subsection (a) shall be made by lot among the judges eligible under subsections (a) and (a-2).** **The absence of a prescribed method does not suspend subsection (a).**\n\n**(d)** This Section governs the assignment of a case within a district. It does not alter venue, the requirements for relief, or the scope of relief a court may afford."},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Publication of Assignment Methods","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 RECUSAL AND CASE ASSIGNMENT","body":"**(a)** Each district court and court of appeals shall publish its case-assignment rules in machine-readable form, and shall report annually to the Administrative Office the number of cases assigned to each judge, by division and by category of case, listing separately each case assigned under \u00a7 7-3(a-2) and the judge who heard it. The Administrative Office shall publish the reports.\n\n**(b) Panel composition.** Each court of appeals shall publish the method by which it composes panels, which shall provide for random selection, and shall report annually the distribution of panel assignments among its judges, listing separately each senior judge and each judge sitting by designation, and the officer or judicial council that designated each. A court may depart from random selection only under a published rule, and shall report each departure and its basis.\n\n**(c) Audit of randomness.** The Administrative Office shall test annually whether actual assignments and panel compositions are consistent with the published random methods, applying a statistical standard it publishes, and shall publish the results and any material deviation."},{"anchor":"sec-7-5","heading":"\u00a7 7-5. Statement of Authority for Relief Beyond the Parties","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 RECUSAL AND CASE ASSIGNMENT","body":"A court granting relief that restrains the enforcement of a statute, regulation, or executive action as to persons who are not parties shall state in its order the source of its authority to afford relief of that scope and the reason such scope is necessary to afford complete relief to the parties before it. This Section requires a statement of reasons. It does not limit the relief a court may grant or create a ground for reversal."},{"anchor":"sec-7-6","heading":"\u00a7 7-6. Conflict Discovered After Decision","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 RECUSAL AND CASE ASSIGNMENT","body":"Where a covered judge learns, after participating in a matter, of a ground that would have required disqualification, the judge shall promptly notify the parties and enter the notice on the docket, and shall state whether the judge divested or otherwise resolved the interest. The parties may seek such relief as the law provides. This Section requires notice; it does not itself create a ground for vacatur or alter the standard governing any motion."},{"anchor":"sec-7-7","heading":"\u00a7 7-7. Cases Are Decided by Judges","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 RECUSAL AND CASE ASSIGNMENT","body":"**(a) The judicial power is exercised by persons.** Every order, judgment, decree, finding of fact, conclusion of law, sentence, and other disposition of a matter in a court of the United States **shall be made by a natural person holding judicial office, or by a jury**, and shall be entered as the act of that person or of that jury. **No automated system may make any such disposition**, and the output of an automated system is not an order, judgment, decree, finding, conclusion, sentence, or disposition.\n\n**(b) What a system may do.** Nothing in this Section limits the use of an automated system to docket, index, search, retrieve, organize, transcribe, translate, summarize, calculate, sort, cite-check, format, schedule, notify, or draft; to assign cases and compose panels as \u00a7\u00a7 7-3 and 7-4 require, and to conduct the audit \u00a7 7-4(c) requires; or to perform any other function in the administration of a court or in the preparation of a disposition. **A use permitted by this subsection is not a ground for vacatur, reversal, recusal, mistrial, or any other relief.**\n\n**(c) No delegation.** The disposition of a matter may not be delegated to an automated system by rule of court, standing order, local rule, assignment, or consent of the parties. A disposition prepared with the assistance of an automated system is the act of the judge who enters it, and the judge is accountable for it as for any other.\n\n**(d) No inquiry into deliberation.** This Section states **who** disposes of a matter. It creates no ground to inquire into how a judge reached a disposition, no duty to disclose a court's internal deliberative process, and no right in any party to discovery, testimony, or disclosure concerning either.\n\n**(e) Other judicial officers.** This Section applies to every covered judge, to every other judicial officer, and to every officer or employee of a court of the United States exercising authority to dispose of a matter, **without regard to \u00a7 4-6.**\n\n**(f) Constitutional basis.** This Section rests on the authority of Congress to constitute tribunals inferior to the Supreme Court and to prescribe the procedure of the courts it establishes. **It states who exercises the judicial power of the United States. It does not direct how any judge decides any matter, and nothing in it is a rule of decision.**\n\n**(g) Severability.** If this Section is held invalid as applied to any court or class of judicial officer, it continues to operate as to every other. If subsection (e) is held invalid, subsections (a) through (d) continue to operate as to covered judges."},{"anchor":"sec-8-1","heading":"\u00a7 8-1. Code of Conduct","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a)** The Code of Conduct for United States Judges, as adopted and amended by the Judicial Conference, applies to every covered judge, including a Justice of the Supreme Court.\n\n**(b)** The Supreme Court may adopt for its own members a code that is more restrictive than the Code of Conduct for United States Judges, but not one that is less restrictive.\n\n**(c)** A code adopted by the Supreme Court shall provide a mechanism by which an allegation of a violation is received, reviewed, and resolved, and by which the resolution is published.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-8-2","heading":"\u00a7 8-2. Review Panel","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a)** Where the Supreme Court has not provided a mechanism under \u00a7 8-1(c), an allegation that a Justice has violated the applicable code is referred to a review panel composed of five judges in active or senior service on the courts of appeals, drawn by lot by the Administrative Office, no two from the same circuit, and none appointed by the President who appointed the Justice in question.\n\n**(b) Fact-finding only.** The panel gathers and states facts. It shall compile a written record of the facts relevant to the allegation and transmit it to the Supreme Court and to the Administrative Office, which shall publish it. The panel shall not determine whether a violation occurred, shall not characterize conduct as a violation, shall not recommend any disposition, and shall not impose or propose any sanction.\n\n**(b-1) Determination by the Court.** Whether the facts constitute a violation, and what if anything follows, is determined by the Supreme Court. The Court shall publish its determination or shall publish the fact that it has made none.\n\n**(b-2) Declination to respond.** Where a Justice declines to respond to an inquiry of the panel, the panel shall record the fact and the Administrative Office shall publish it. No adverse inference is drawn, and publication of the fact is the only consequence.\n\n**(c) No sanction.** The panel's record carries no sanction, does not bind the Supreme Court or any Justice, and does not affect the tenure, compensation, assignment, or participation of any Justice in any matter. Its operative effect is publication of facts.\n\n**(d) Merits excluded.** The panel shall not review the merits of any decision, order, vote, or opinion, and shall not inquire into the deliberations of the Court.\n\n**(e) Superseded by the Court's own mechanism.** This Section has no application where the Supreme Court has adopted a mechanism under \u00a7 8-1(c)."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3","heading":"\u00a7 8-3. Disclosure Timelines","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"A Justice of the Supreme Court files each report required by this Act and by the Ethics in Government Act on the same schedule, and subject to the same extensions, as every other covered judge. The Administrative Office shall publish the date of each filing and of each amendment, together with a statement of the period between the reportable event and its disclosure."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4","heading":"\u00a7 8-4. Transparency of Orders","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a)** Every order of the Supreme Court disposing of an application or a petition, including an order granting or denying a stay, an injunction, or vacatur, shall record the vote of each Justice, identify any Justice not participating, and identify the author of any opinion or statement accompanying the order.\n\n**(b)** Subsection (a) applies whether or not the order is accompanied by an opinion, and whether or not the matter was set for argument.\n\n**(b-1) Summary dispositions included.** Subsection (a) applies to a summary disposition of any kind \u2014 including an order granting certiorari, vacating, and remanding; a summary affirmance or reversal; and a dismissal as improvidently granted \u2014 and the order shall identify the ground on which the disposition rests, stated at least by reference to the decision, event, or rule in whose light it is entered.\n\n**(c)** This Section requires the recording of votes and participation. It does not require the Court to state reasons, does not prescribe the form or content of any opinion, and does not affect the disposition of any matter."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4a","heading":"\u00a7 8-4A. Standards for Emergency Applications","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a) Rule required.** The Supreme Court shall adopt, publish, and follow written standards governing the disposition of an application for a stay, injunction, or vacatur presented other than on the merits docket.\n\n**(b) Subjects addressed.** The standards shall address: the showing required of an applicant; the circumstances warranting expedited disposition; the ordinary period allowed for response; and the form in which a disposition is entered. Congress prescribes the subjects the standards must address and does not prescribe their content.\n\n**(c) Reservation.** Nothing in this Section prescribes the standard governing any application, limits the Court's discretion to grant or deny relief, restricts the Court's authority to act immediately, or subjects any disposition to review.\n\n**(d) Existing rules suffice.** Standards already adopted and published that address the subjects in subsection (b) satisfy this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4b","heading":"\u00a7 8-4B. Emergency Applications by the United States","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a) Contents.** An application for a stay, injunction pending appeal, or other emergency relief filed in any court of the United States by or on behalf of the United States, or of an agency or officer of the United States, shall identify \u2014\n\n**(1)** the specific statutory or constitutional authority relied upon;\n\n**(2)** the contemporaneous written statement, if any, of the factual and legal basis for the action from which relief is sought, made at the time the action was taken, or a statement that none was made; and\n\n**(3)** the irreparable injury asserted, stated with particularity.\n\n**(b) Publication.** The applicant shall publish an application filed under subsection (a), and any supplement to it, not later than the period set in the schedule to this Act after filing, except to the extent that publication would disclose properly classified information or information whose disclosure another statute prohibits.\n\n**(c) Reservation.** This Section imposes no requirement upon any court and prescribes no standard for the disposition of any application. \u00a7 8-4A regulates what the Court adopts; this Section regulates what the government's application contains, and the two operate independently."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4c","heading":"\u00a7 8-4C. Certification by a Majority of a Circuit","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a) The dormant lever, revived.** Where a court of appeals certifies a question of law to the Supreme Court under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 1254(2), and the certification is made by a **recorded and published vote of a majority of the judges of that court in regular active service**, the Supreme Court **shall decide the question so certified** \u2014 by giving binding instructions or by requiring the entire record and deciding the whole matter, as that section provides \u2014 and shall not decline the certification as a matter of discretion.\n\n**(b) Reservation.** This Section makes the Court's jurisdiction over a majority-certified question obligatory rather than discretionary, which is a regulation of appellate jurisdiction. It prescribes no answer, no reasoning, no timetable, and no disposition, and a certification made otherwise than by the majority vote this Section describes is governed by 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 1254(2) as it stands.\n\n**(c) Symmetry stated.** This Section operates identically whichever judges compose a majority of any circuit, and the certification it makes obligatory is available to every circuit on the same terms."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4d","heading":"\u00a7 8-4D. Reporting of the Emergency Docket","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a) The report.** The Supreme Court shall compile, and the Administrative Office shall publish, for each term, in machine-readable form, a report of every application presented other than on the merits docket, stating for each \u2014\n\n**(1)** the date filed and the date of disposition, **and the interval between them**;\n\n**(2)** whether the applicant is the United States, an agency or officer of the United States, or any other person;\n\n**(3)** the relief sought, and whether it was granted, denied, or otherwise resolved, **including a disposition by the expiration or mootness of the application without action**;\n\n**(4)** whether the disposition was accompanied by a statement of reasons, by an opinion, or by neither; and\n\n**(5)** the vote as recorded under \u00a7 8-4.\n\n**(b) Categories, not conclusions.** This Section prescribes the categories the report shall state and no more. The categories are a minimum, and the Court may publish more. **The report characterizes nothing**; its arithmetic is the public's to do.\n\n**(c) Consistency.** The report shall apply the same categories in each term, and a change in how a category is counted shall be stated with the report."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4e","heading":"\u00a7 8-4E. Statement Accompanying an Order Affecting a Statute's Operation","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a) Scope.** This Section applies to an order of the Supreme Court, entered other than on the merits docket, that stays, enjoins, or restores the operation or enforcement of a Federal or State statute, or that stays or vacates a lower court's order doing so.\n\n**(b) The statement.** An order to which this Section applies shall be accompanied by a statement identifying \u2014 **what the order accomplishes as to the statute's operation; the authority for affording relief of that kind; and the factors the Court applied.** The statement may be brief, and nothing in this Section prescribes its content, requires any particular factor, or conditions the order's effect on the statement's adequacy.\n\n**(c) The absence is recorded, and counted.** An order entered without the statement is effective according to its terms. **The report under \u00a7 8-4D shall record, for each order to which this Section applies, whether the statement was made** \u2014 the absence is recorded rather than presumed, in the manner of \u00a7 7-2(c)."},{"anchor":"sec-8-5","heading":"\u00a7 8-5. Public Access to Proceedings","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a)** The Supreme Court and each court of appeals shall provide live audio transmission of each argument open to the public, and shall make a recording and a transcript publicly available without charge.\n\n**(b)** A court may withhold or delay transmission in a particular matter upon a finding, entered on the docket, that transmission would create a substantial risk to a person's safety or would prejudice the fair administration of the matter.\n\n**(c)** Nothing in this Section requires video transmission, and the decision whether to permit video remains with the court."},{"anchor":"sec-8-6","heading":"\u00a7 8-6. Public Access to Records","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"Electronic access to the records and docket of any Federal court shall be provided to the public without charge. The Administrative Office shall provide bulk access in structured, machine-readable form, with documented fields for parties, counsel, judge, assignment date, docket events, and disposition, so that the record is analyzable and not merely readable."},{"anchor":"sec-8-6a","heading":"\u00a7 8-6A. The Record Belongs to the Court","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a) A filing becomes the record.** A document filed with a Federal court becomes part of the record of that court. **A party may not withdraw, seal, replace, or remove from public view a document it has filed**, and no such act by a party has any effect on the public availability of the document.\n\n**(b) Sealing is an application.** Relief from public availability may be obtained **only on application to the court**, determined by the court, and entered on the docket. The application shall state the interest asserted and the portions to which it extends, and **the court shall seal no more than that interest requires.**\n\n**(c) Withdrawal by the filer.** A party may move to withdraw a filing. **Until the court rules, the document remains part of the record and publicly available**, and an order permitting withdrawal does not remove the document from the record unless the court so orders on the standard stated in subsection (b).\n\n**(d) Government filings.** Subsections (a) through (c) apply to the United States and to every officer and agency of the United States **on the same terms as to any other party.**\n\n**(d-1) Material a statute protects.** Nothing in this Section requires the public availability of matter occurring before a grand jury, properly classified national security information, or any matter a statute requires to be sealed or prohibits from disclosure. **Where a filing contains such matter, the application under subsection (b) shall extend only to that matter.**\n\n**(e) Docket entries are not deleted.** Where a document is sealed or withdrawn, **the docket shall continue to reflect that it was filed, the date of filing, and the disposition of the application**, so that the fact of the filing remains ascertainable from the record."},{"anchor":"sec-8-7","heading":"\u00a7 8-7. Notice of Senior Status and Retirement","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 CODE OF CONDUCT AND SUPREME COURT TRANSPARENCY","body":"A covered judge who elects senior status, retires, or resigns shall file notice with the Administrative Office, which shall publish the notice, its date, and the effective date of the change, in the database under Section 3."},{"anchor":"sec-9-1","heading":"\u00a7 9-1. Application of Civil Rights and Employment Law","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 WORKPLACE PROTECTIONS IN THE JUDICIARY","body":"**(a)** Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Age Discrimination in Employment Act of 1967, the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, the Family and Medical Leave Act of 1993, and the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 apply to an employee of the judicial branch of the United States, including a law clerk, a chambers employee, an employee of a court, an employee of the Administrative Office, and an employee of the Federal Judicial Center, and to an applicant for any such position.\n\n**(b)** An employee covered by subsection (a) has the rights, remedies, and procedures available under the applicable statute, including a civil action, subject to \u00a7 9-2.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 9 \u2014 WORKPLACE PROTECTIONS IN THE JUDICIARY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-9-2","heading":"\u00a7 9-2. Forum and Procedure","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 WORKPLACE PROTECTIONS IN THE JUDICIARY","body":"**(a)** A civil action under \u00a7 9-1 is brought in a district court outside the circuit in which the employing court sits, assigned by random selection made by the Administrative Office and not by any court within the affected circuit, and heard by a judge from outside that circuit sitting by designation. **Until the Administrative Office has published a procedure for assignment under this subsection, assignment is made by lot among the district courts outside the affected circuit, and the absence of a published procedure delays no action.**\n\n**(c) Jury trial.** Where the statute under which the action arises provides a right to trial by jury, that right applies to an action under \u00a7 9-1. A claim that would be tried to a jury if brought against any other Federal employer is tried to a jury when brought against the judiciary."},{"anchor":"sec-9-3","heading":"\u00a7 9-3. Office of Judicial Workplace Integrity","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 WORKPLACE PROTECTIONS IN THE JUDICIARY","body":"**(a)** There is established, within the judicial branch, an Office of Judicial Workplace Integrity, headed by a Director appointed by the Judicial Conference for a single fixed term of 8 years. The Office receives complaints, protects the identity of those who make them, compiles and publishes data, and makes referrals. It does not adjudicate, does not enforce, and exercises no authority to impose any consequence on any person.\n\n**(a-1) Removal.** The Director may be removed before the expiration of the term only for inefficiency, neglect of duty, or malfeasance in office, on a written statement of the grounds, which shall be published.\n\n**(a-2) Independent budget and publication.** The Director shall prepare the Office's budget request, which the Judicial Conference shall transmit without revision. The Director shall publish the Office's annual report directly.\n\n**(b) Function.** The Office shall receive complaints of conduct described in \u00a7 9-1 or \u00a7 9-4(a) from any judicial-branch employee or applicant, from any circuit; shall assist the complainant in understanding the remedies available under \u00a7 9-1 and \u00a7 9-2; shall refer a complaint, at the complainant's election, to the judicial council of a circuit other than that in which the subject sits, or to the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission where that agency has jurisdiction under the applicable statute; and shall compile and publish data under subsection (d).\n\n**(b-1) No enforcement authority.** The Office conducts no adjudication, issues no findings of violation, imposes no sanction, and compels no person. Enforcement of a right under \u00a7 9-1 is by civil action under \u00a7 9-2.\n\n**(c)** A complaint may be filed with the Office regardless of the circuit in which the employee works, and shall not be referred to the circuit in which the subject of the complaint sits.\n\n**(d)** The Office shall report annually, in aggregate and without identifying any individual, the number of complaints received, their categories, their dispositions, and the time to disposition, by circuit.\n\n**(e) Anonymity.** The Office shall protect the identity of a reporting employee throughout an investigation, and shall not disclose it to the subject of the complaint or to the employing court without the employee's written consent, except where disclosure is unavoidable to afford the subject the process due and the employee has been notified and given the opportunity to withdraw the complaint. The Office shall maintain a channel permitting anonymous reporting."},{"anchor":"sec-9-4","heading":"\u00a7 9-4. Protection of Reporting Employees","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 WORKPLACE PROTECTIONS IN THE JUDICIARY","body":"**(a)** No judicial-branch employee shall be discharged, demoted, reassigned, denied a recommendation or reference, or otherwise retaliated against for reporting, or for participating in an investigation of, conduct the employee reasonably believes violates this Act, the applicable code of conduct, chapter 16 of title 28, or any law.\n\n**(b)** An employee subjected to retaliation has a civil action, brought under \u00a7 9-2, for reinstatement, back pay, compensatory damages, and costs.\n\n**(c)** A judge shall not, in retaliation for a report, decline to provide a reference or provide a negative reference.\n\n**(d) Record of service.** The Administrative Office shall maintain, for each law clerk and chambers employee, a record of the fact and dates of service and of the completion of the term, available to the employee and to any person the employee designates. The record states service and nothing more."},{"anchor":"sec-9-5","heading":"\u00a7 9-5. Confidentiality Agreements","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 WORKPLACE PROTECTIONS IN THE JUDICIARY","body":"Any provision of an employment agreement, clerkship agreement, confidentiality agreement, separation agreement, or judicial-branch policy is void and unenforceable to the extent it prohibits or discourages an employee from reporting conduct described in \u00a7 9-4(a) to the Office of Judicial Workplace Integrity, to a judicial council, to Congress, or to a law-enforcement authority. Confidentiality as to the deliberations of a court, the drafting of an opinion, and the substance of a pending matter is not affected by this Section."},{"anchor":"sec-9-6","heading":"\u00a7 9-6. Training and Notice","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 WORKPLACE PROTECTIONS IN THE JUDICIARY","body":"Each covered judge and each judicial-branch employee shall complete annual training on workplace conduct and on the rights and procedures under this Section, and the Administrative Office shall publish completion rates by court. Each employee shall receive written notice of the rights conferred by this Section upon hiring."},{"anchor":"sec-9-7","heading":"\u00a7 9-7. Exit Surveys and Climate Data","part":"SECTION 9 \u2014 WORKPLACE PROTECTIONS IN THE JUDICIARY","body":"The Office of Judicial Workplace Integrity shall conduct a confidential survey of each departing law clerk and chambers employee, and a periodic climate survey of judicial-branch employees, and shall publish the results in aggregate by circuit, in a form that does not identify any individual or chambers."},{"anchor":"sec-10-1","heading":"\u00a7 10-1. Disclosure by Amici","part":"SECTION 10 \u2014 AMICUS AND NOMINATION TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a)** A brief filed as amicus curiae in any Federal court shall disclose: each person who contributed to the preparation or submission of the brief; each person who contributed to the amicus, in the twelve months preceding the filing, an amount above a threshold set in the schedule to this Act, where the contribution was earmarked for or reasonably attributable to the amicus's litigation activity; and whether any such person has an interest in the outcome of the matter.\n\n**(a-1) Purpose or direction, not presumption.** In addition to a contributor described in subsection (a), an amicus shall disclose any person who, within the twelve months preceding the filing, contributed above the threshold **for the purpose of** the litigation in which the brief is filed, or who **directed, requested, or was consulted concerning** the filing of the brief or its content. No presumption of attribution applies, and a general contribution to the amicus is not disclosable by reason of the amicus's litigation activity alone.\n\n**(a-2) Common funding across briefs.** Where two or more amici filing in the same matter received, within the twelve months preceding the filing, contributions above the threshold from the same person, each such brief shall disclose that fact and identify the common contributor.\n\n**(b)** Where the amicus is an organization that does not disclose its contributors generally, subsection (a) requires disclosure only of contributions described in that subsection and subsection (a-1), and not of the organization's general membership or donor list.\n\n**(c)** A court may excuse disclosure of a contributor upon a showing of a reasonable probability that disclosure would subject the contributor to threats, harassment, or reprisal.\n\n**(c-1) The basis of this Section.** This Section is a condition on the voluntary submission of a brief to a court of the United States, in the manner of Rule 29 of the Federal Rules of Appellate Procedure, which already requires an amicus to disclose whether a party or its counsel contributed to the brief. It is not a compelled-disclosure requirement imposed on an association by reason of its existence or its advocacy generally, and it applies only to a person who chooses to address a tribunal. Neither the exemption in subsection (c) nor any confidentiality afforded to a filing is offered in justification of this Section.\n\n**(d)** This Section requires disclosure. It does not limit who may file, what a brief may say, or how much any person may contribute.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 10 \u2014 AMICUS AND NOMINATION TRANSPARENCY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-10-2","heading":"\u00a7 10-2. Disclosure of Spending on Judicial Nominations","part":"SECTION 10 \u2014 AMICUS AND NOMINATION TRANSPARENCY","body":"**(a)** A person who makes disbursements above a threshold set in the schedule to this Act, in a calendar year, for **public communications that support or oppose an identified nominee** to a Federal court, shall file with the Federal Election Commission a statement identifying the disbursements and each person who contributed above that threshold **for the purpose of those communications**.\n\n**(b)** The statement is published in machine-readable form.\n\n**(c) What this Section does not reach.** This Section does not reach a contribution to an organization by reason of the organization's engagement in nomination advocacy among its other activities; a communication that does not identify a nominee; advocacy concerning the composition of a court in general terms; or any communication to the President, to a Senator, or to any officer of the United States. It reaches public communications about an identified person, and the funds given for the purpose of making them.\n\n**(d) Disclosure only.** This Section imposes no limit on the amount any person may spend or contribute for any purpose.\n\n**(e) Exemption for threat of harassment.** The Commission shall exempt a person from disclosure under this Section on a showing of a reasonable probability that disclosure would subject the person or the person's family to threats, harassment, or reprisal. The exemption is not offered in justification of the obligation, which rests on the public's interest in knowing who is paying for a public campaign about the appointment of a judge who will hold office during good behavior."},{"anchor":"sec-10-3","heading":"\u00a7 10-3. Relationship Disclosure","part":"SECTION 10 \u2014 AMICUS AND NOMINATION TRANSPARENCY","body":"Where a person required to be disclosed under \u00a7 10-1 or \u00a7 10-2 appears as a party, counsel, or amicus in a matter before a covered judge, the disclosure shall so state, and the judge shall consider the relationship under 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455. This Section imposes a disclosure obligation and does not itself create a ground for disqualification."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-0","heading":"\u00a7 10A-0. Definitions for This Section","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) Judicial officer.** For purposes of this Section, **\"judicial officer\"** means a covered judge and an other judicial officer, as those terms are defined in \u00a7 0-3, and a person who formerly held either office.\n\n**(b) Judicial employee.** **\"Judicial employee\"** means an officer or employee of the judicial branch of the United States, including an employee of a court, of the Administrative Office, and of the Federal Judicial Center.\n\n**(c) Household.** A **\"member of the household\"** of a judicial officer or judicial employee is a person who, with respect to that officer or employee, is described in section 5933(1) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022.\n\n**(d) A threat arising from the performance of judicial duties.** A threat **arises from the performance of judicial duties** if the performance of those duties, or the holding of the office, **is a reason for the threat, whether or not the sole reason, the principal reason, or a reason the person making the threat states.** **No other causal standard applies to this Section**, and the omission of any other standard is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(d-1) Concerning behavior.** **\"Concerning behavior\"** means communication or conduct indicating an unusual or inappropriate interest in a judicial officer, a judicial employee, a member of the household of either, or a court \u2014 **including a threat, an approach, surveillance or the gathering of information about a person's residence or movements, a fixation on a person or on a matter, a grievance pursued beyond the means the law provides for pursuing it, and conduct indicating preparation for an attack.** A threat is one form of concerning behavior **and is not the paradigm of it**; concerning behavior may be present where no threat has been made, and the absence of a threat is not a reason to find that concerning behavior is absent. The enumeration is illustrative, and the omission of any conduct from it is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(e) Protection.** **\"Protection\"** includes a protective detail; residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance; protective intelligence and threat monitoring; and any other measure taken to protect a person from physical harm. The enumeration is illustrative and does not limit the measures an assessment under \u00a7 10A-4 may specify.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-10a-1","heading":"\u00a7 10A-1. Determination of the Security Requirements of the Judicial Branch","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 566(i) of title 28, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(i)** The Director of the United States Marshals Service shall consult with the Judicial Conference of the United States on a continuing basis regarding the security requirements for the judicial branch of the United States Government~~, to ensure that the views of the Judicial Conference regarding the security requirements for the judicial branch of the Federal Government are taken into account when determining staffing levels, setting priorities for programs regarding judicial security, and allocating judicial security resources~~. In this paragraph, the term \"judicial security\" includes the security of buildings housing the judiciary, the personal security of judicial officers, the assessment of threats made to judicial officers, and the protection of all other judicial personnel. ~~The United States Marshals Service retains final authority regarding security requirements for the judicial branch of the Federal Government.~~ **The Judicial Conference of the United States shall adopt and publish the criteria by which the security requirements of the judicial branch of the Federal Government are determined, including criteria governing staffing levels, priorities among programs regarding judicial security, and the allocation of judicial security resources; and the Administrative Office of the United States Courts shall determine those requirements by applying the published criteria. The United States Marshals Service retains authority over the means and manner by which its own personnel execute a requirement so determined, and over every other function committed to it by law.**\n\n**(b) What is published, and what is not.** The Judicial Conference shall publish the criteria adopted under section 566(i) of title 28, United States Code, and each amendment to them, **withholding nothing except as subsection (b-2) provides.** **A determination applying those criteria to an identified person, court, or location is not published**, and is effective upon being made and transmitted to the United States Marshals Service. The Administrative Office shall publish annually the number of determinations made and the categories of protection they required, without identifying any person, court, or location.\n\n**(b-1) Until criteria are adopted.** **The absence of criteria adopted under subsection (a) does not suspend this Section, \u00a7 10A-2A, \u00a7 10A-3, or \u00a7 10A-4.** Until criteria are adopted and published, a determination under subsection (a) is made on the security standards the United States Marshals Service applies in practice, which the Administrative Office shall obtain and publish, withholding nothing except as subsection (b-2) provides, and a determination so made has every effect of a determination made under adopted criteria.\n\n**(b-2) Withholding only what would jeopardize safety, and the withholding tested.**\n\n**(1) The standard.** The Judicial Conference in a publication under subsection (b), the Administrative Office in a publication under subsection (b-1), and any officer, office, or body of the United States publishing the written criteria under which an assessment or a screen is performed under \u00a7 10A-4(a), including under \u00a7 11-3(f), **shall withhold** from the publication a specific part of the criteria **where, and only where, its publication could reasonably be expected to jeopardize the safety of a judicial officer, a judicial employee, or a member of the household of either** \u2014 including by enabling a person to avoid detection by an assessment or a screen under \u00a7 10A-4 or by a case under \u00a7 10A-4B, to keep conduct below what would bring a higher level of protection, or to defeat the protection a determination or an assessment specifies. **Each level of protection under \u00a7 10A-4(b-1), and the kinds of measure it comprises, is always published.** That publication would show which persons, courts, or locations, or which classes of them, receive protection, would expose the criteria or any body making, publishing, or applying them to criticism, or would invite litigation is never a ground for withholding, and does not defeat a withholding this paragraph otherwise requires.\n\n**(2) The withholding is published.** For each part withheld, the body making the publication shall publish, with the criteria or the amendment from which it is withheld, **that a part has been withheld, the part of the criteria to which it belongs, the subject it addresses, and the reason its publication could reasonably be expected to jeopardize safety, each stated as specifically as paragraph (1) permits.**\n\n**(3) Withholding permits nothing this Section forbids.** A part withheld is subject to \u00a7 10A-4(b-1A), \u00a7 10A-4(b-2), \u00a7 10A-4(e), and \u00a7 10A-4B(c-1) and (c-2) as published criteria are, and its withholding is not a ground for applying any consideration those provisions forbid.\n\n**(4) Who holds it, and how it is kept.** The body that withholds a part shall furnish it to the United States Marshals Service, to the Administrative Office, and to each person who performs an assessment under \u00a7 10A-4(a-1), upon the procurement, and each of them applies it as the published criteria are applied. The body that withholds a part and each of them shall implement and maintain reasonable security procedures and practices to protect a part withheld from unauthorized use, disclosure, access, destruction, or modification. **The duties of the Marshals Service under this paragraph arise from this subsection and not from any command of the Judicial Conference.** A part withheld, while it is withheld under this subsection, is specifically exempted from disclosure by statute for purposes of section 552(b)(3) of title 5, United States Code, and shall be withheld under that section in such a manner as to leave no discretion on the issue.\n\n**(5) Reliance on it is tested.** A determination under subsection (a), and a finding under \u00a7 10A-4, that rests in any part on a part withheld shall so state, identifying it by the description published under paragraph (2). In an action under \u00a7 10A-4(f-1), **each part withheld on which a determination or finding rests shall be furnished to the court and to counsel for the plaintiff, under such protective order as the court enters.** The court determines de novo, and may examine the part in camera, whether its withholding meets paragraph (1) and whether paragraph (2) was complied with. Where paragraph (1) is not met, the court shall order the part published; where only paragraph (2) was not complied with, the court shall order the publication paragraph (2) requires, and not publication of the part.\n\n**(6) Open to challenge whether or not relied on.** **A judicial officer, a judicial employee, or a member of the household of either may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States to compel publication of a part withheld otherwise than as paragraph (1) permits, or of a statement paragraph (2) requires that has not been made**, and is entitled to costs and reasonable attorney's fees on prevailing. The action is brought against the Director of the Administrative Office, or, while that office is vacant, the officer performing its functions, in that officer's official capacity, who makes any publication the court orders. **The United States waives its sovereign immunity from the action as to the relief this paragraph provides, including costs and reasonable attorney's fees, and as to no other relief**, and the district courts of the United States have jurisdiction of it under section 1331 of title 28, United States Code. The court determines the question de novo, may examine the part in camera, shall order publication of a part whose withholding paragraph (1) does not permit, and shall order the publication paragraph (2) requires where it was not made. **A failure to comply with paragraph (2) alone is never a ground for ordering a part published.** **A person who may bring an action under this paragraph and is denied publication this subsection requires is injured by the denial**: the criteria decide whether, and at what level, that person is protected, and whether a reduction of that protection can be tested under \u00a7 10A-4(f-1). The action is on the plaintiff's own entitlement, is not brought on behalf of the United States, assigns no claim of the United States, and does not require the participation, consent, or non-opposition of the Attorney General or of any other officer.\n\n**(7) A failed withholding costs the protected person nothing.** A failure to furnish a part withheld as paragraph (5) requires, or to comply with paragraph (1) or (2), operates against a determination or finding only so far as it denies, lowers, delays, or ends protection, and to that extent the part is not part of the published criteria in that action and the determination or finding did not apply the published criteria. **It never defeats a level of protection a determination or an assessment has specified, and never makes the rest of the criteria, or any determination or assessment made under them, other than published.**\n\n**(8) Criteria so published are published criteria.** Criteria published under subsection (b), under subsection (b-1), or for purposes of \u00a7 10A-4(a), with a part withheld under this subsection, are criteria published, and criteria published in advance, for every purpose of this Section, including \u00a7\u00a7 10A-3, 10A-4, and 10A-4B, of \u00a7 11-3(f), and of section 566(i) of title 28, United States Code, as amended by subsection (a), and a determination or an assessment made under them is made under published criteria.\n\n**(c) No direction of any person outside the judicial branch.** A determination under this Section states what the judicial branch requires. **It directs no officer or employee of the executive branch, compels no appointment, removal, assignment, or expenditure by any such officer, and confers no authority on the Judicial Conference or the Administrative Office over any person outside the judicial branch.** The obligation of the Marshals Service under \u00a7 10A-3 arises from that Section and not from any command of the Conference."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-2","heading":"\u00a7 10A-2. Authority of the Judiciary to Procure Its Own Protection","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 604(a)(22) of title 28, United States Code, is amended to read as follows:\n\n> **(22)** Receive and expend, either directly or by transfer to the United States Marshals Service or other Government agency, funds appropriated for the procurement, installation, and maintenance of security equipment and protective services for the United States Courts ~~in courtrooms and adjacent areas~~ **, and for the personal protection of judicial officers, judicial employees, and members of their households, wherever located,** including building ingress/egress control, inspection of packages, directed security patrols, **residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance; protective details; and contracts for protective personnel,** and other similar activities;\n\n**(b) Contracts and agreements.** The Administrative Office may contract for protective personnel and protective services, and may enter into a memorandum of understanding with a State or local law enforcement agency for the performance of protection determined to be required under \u00a7 10A-1.\n\n**(c) Cumulative.** The authority of this Section is in addition to every authority of the Marshals Service and displaces none of it."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-2a","heading":"\u00a7 10A-2A. Protective Authority of Personnel Performing Protection","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) The authority.** A person employed by the Administrative Office, or engaged under a contract or agreement under \u00a7 10A-2(b), to perform protection determined to be required under \u00a7 10A-1 shall have authority, in accordance with regulations prescribed by the Director of the Administrative Office and approved by the Judicial Conference \u2014\n\n**(1)** in any location, to protect a person entitled to protection under \u00a7 10A-3 or \u00a7 10A-4;\n\n**(2)** while performing duties necessary to carry out paragraph (1), to make arrests for any violation of Federal or State law and any regulation under Federal or State law; and\n\n**(3)** to carry firearms while performing duties under paragraph (1).\n\n**(b) A detail, and not a police force.** The authority conferred by subsection (a) is exercisable **only while the person is performing the protection of a person under paragraph (a)(1)**. This Section confers **no authority to police a building, grounds, a street, or any other area; no general law enforcement jurisdiction; no authority to patrol, investigate, or search except as necessary to the protection of a person under paragraph (a)(1); and no authority over any person except as paragraph (a)(2) provides.** The omission from this Section of an area-policing authority of the kind conferred by section 6121(a)(1) of title 40, United States Code, **is a determination of Congress and not a gap**, and no such authority is to be implied from the grant of paragraph (a)(2) or (a)(3).\n\n**(c) Standards.** Regulations under subsection (a) shall prescribe qualification, training, and use-of-force standards not less protective of the public than those applicable to a Deputy United States Marshal performing protective duties, and shall be published.\n\n**(c-1) Until regulations are prescribed.** **The absence of regulations under subsection (a) does not suspend the authority conferred by that subsection.** Until regulations are prescribed and published, that authority is exercisable subject to the qualification, training, and use-of-force standards applicable to a Deputy United States Marshal performing protective duties, which the Administrative Office shall obtain and publish.\n\n**(d) Independent of any executive act.** The authority conferred by subsection (a) **does not depend on a deputation, designation, approval, or agreement by any officer of the executive branch**, and does not lapse by the expiration or non-renewal of one. Nothing in this Section limits the deputation of any person under section 0.112 of title 28, Code of Federal Regulations, or under any other regulation or authority of the Attorney General.\n\n**(e) Accountability.** A person exercising authority under this Section is subject to the same duties, restrictions, and liabilities, and the United States to the same liability for that person's acts, as apply to a Deputy United States Marshal performing the same act. The Administrative Office shall publish annually the number of arrests made and the number of uses of force under this Section, by category, without identifying any protected person, court, or location.\n\n**(f) Fallback.** If subsection (a) is held invalid or unenforceable **by a final judgment from which no appeal lies**, and to the extent of that holding, the Director of the United States Marshals Service **shall deputize, on the authority of this subsection, each person engaged under \u00a7 10A-2 to perform protection determined to be required under \u00a7 10A-1, and shall not permit a deputation so made to expire or lapse while the requirement stands**, notwithstanding any regulation requiring a deputation to expire on a date certain. This subsection is severable from subsection (a) and operates only upon such a holding."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-3","heading":"\u00a7 10A-3. Supply, or State the Reason","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) The duty.** Where the Judicial Conference has determined a security requirement under \u00a7 10A-1 and published it, the United States Marshals Service **shall supply** the protection so required, within the period set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(b) Where it is not supplied.** Where the Marshals Service has not supplied a published requirement within that period, the Administrative Office may procure the protection under \u00a7 10A-2, and the Marshals Service shall transmit, within the period set in the schedule to this Act, a statement identifying the requirement not supplied, the extent to which it was not supplied, and the reason.\n\n**(b-1) To whom the statement goes.** A statement under subsection (b) is transmitted to the Judicial Conference, to the Administrative Office, and to the Committees on Appropriations of the Senate and of the House of Representatives. **It is not published, and it is exempt from disclosure under section 552 of title 5, United States Code.** The Administrative Office shall publish annually the number of requirements not supplied within the period and the aggregate duration of non-supply, without identifying any person, court, or location.\n\n**(b-2) What does not satisfy subsection (b).** A statement that does not identify the specific requirement not supplied, the extent to which it was not supplied, and **the personnel, funds, or authority that would be necessary to supply it**, does not satisfy subsection (b), and the requirement is treated as not supplied and unexplained. A statement attributing non-supply to competing priorities without identifying what would be necessary to supply the requirement does not satisfy subsection (b).\n\n**(b-3) Who pays when the Service does not supply.** Where the Administrative Office procures protection under subsection (b), and the protection procured is protection **an assessment under \u00a7 10A-4 has found to be required**, the cost of the procurement is **charged to the appropriation of the United States Marshals Service and transferred to the Administrative Office**. This subsection does not apply to protection required by a determination under \u00a7 10A-1 that no assessment under \u00a7 10A-4 supports, the cost of which is borne as \u00a7 10A-9 provides. **This subsection is the authority for the charge and for the transfer, no further appropriation or authorization is required for either, and no provision of title 31, United States Code, or of an appropriations Act is to be construed to require one.**\n\n**(b-4) How the transfer is made, and how it is contested.** The Administrative Office shall certify to the Secretary of the Treasury the amount **actually expended** on a procurement to which subsection (b-3) applies, and the Secretary shall make the transfer. **The Secretary's duty is ministerial.** The United States Marshals Service may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States contesting whether subsection (b-3) applies to a procurement or whether the amount certified was actually expended, and **the court shall determine those questions and no others**; the transfer is not stayed by the action, and an amount determined not to have been properly charged is returned.\n\n**(c) The statement is not a condition.** The duty under subsection (a) is not conditioned upon, suspended by, or discharged by a statement under subsection (b), and the Administrative Office's authority under subsection (b) does not depend on the statement having been made.\n\n**(d) Procurement does not relieve.** Protection procured by the Administrative Office under subsection (b) satisfies the requirement as to the person protected and does not relieve the Marshals Service of the duty under subsection (a) as to any other requirement."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-4","heading":"\u00a7 10A-4. Threat Assessment, and What Follows From It","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) Assessment.** The United States Marshals Service shall perform a threat assessment **as to a judicial officer, a judicial employee, or a member of the household of either** \u2014 upon the request of that person, **upon concerning behavior** relating to that person reported to the Marshals Service or to the Administrative Office, upon referral by the Administrative Office, or upon its own initiative \u2014 within the period set in the schedule to this Act; and shall perform, as to each judicial officer at the interval set in the schedule to this Act, **a screen of the records the Marshals Service and the Administrative Office already hold** \u2014 including threat reports, the monitoring conducted under section 5936(a)(1) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022, and any prior assessment \u2014 **which becomes a full assessment as to any officer the screen identifies.** **As to a person receiving protection under subsection (b), the periodic duty is a full assessment and not a screen**, so that protection in effect is revisited rather than merely carried forward. Each assessment and each screen is performed under written criteria, published in advance, **withholding nothing except as \u00a7 10A-1(b-2) provides.**\n\n**(a-0) Assessment on request.** The periodic screen under subsection (a) does not narrow who is entitled to an assessment on request under that subsection.\n\n**(a-1) Where no assessment is performed.** Where an assessment has not been performed within the period required by subsection (a), the Administrative Office may perform it or procure its performance under the published criteria, and an assessment so performed has the same effect under this Section as one performed by the Marshals Service.\n\n**(a-2) Where the clock is missed, protection does not wait.** Where an assessment has been requested under subsection (a) and none has been performed within the period that subsection requires, the person who requested it shall receive, pending the assessment, **protection at level (2) of \u00a7 10A-4(b-1)** \u2014 residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance \u2014 and, **where the request is accompanied by concerning behavior reported to a law enforcement agency or through the channel under subsection (a-4), at level (3)**. **No higher level is available under this subsection**. Protection under this subsection continues until an assessment performed under the published criteria determines the level required, and **the failure to perform an assessment is never a ground for withholding protection under this subsection.**\n\n**(a-3) Capacity is published, and a shortfall is priced.** The Administrative Office shall publish annually the number of assessments requested, the number performed within the period required by subsection (a), the number that produced protection under subsection (a-2), and the number of positions applied to the assessment function. **Where the proportion performed within that period falls below the standard set in the schedule to this Act, the publication shall state the number of additional positions required to meet it and the amount necessary to fund them.**\n\n**(a-4) The channel.** The Administrative Office shall maintain, and publish, a channel by which a judicial officer, a judicial employee, a member of the household of either, a court, or **a State or local law enforcement agency** may report **concerning behavior**, and shall transmit each report to the Marshals Service on receipt. **The channel shall accept a report of conduct the reporting person cannot characterize**, and shall not require the reporting person to state that the conduct is a threat or to assess it in any way. **Concerning behavior reported to a State or local law enforcement agency and not transmitted to the Marshals Service or the Administrative Office does not trigger subsection (a)**. The Administrative Office shall invite each State and local law enforcement agency to use the channel, and shall publish annually the number of reports received through it and their sources in aggregate.\n\n**(b) Protection follows the assessment.** Where an assessment finds that a judicial officer, a judicial employee, or a member of the household of either faces a threat arising from the performance of judicial duties, **that person shall receive protection at the level the assessment specifies.** No further determination by any officer is a condition of the protection.\n\n**(b-1) The levels, from least to greatest.** The levels of protection are \u2014\n\n**(1)** the measures \u00a7 10A-4A makes available without an assessment, together with protective-intelligence monitoring;\n\n**(2)** residential security equipment and its installation, monitoring, and maintenance;\n\n**(3)** presence at a residence, a courthouse, or a place the person is to be, periodically or on a stated occasion;\n\n**(4)** a protective detail for a stated period or occasion; and\n\n**(5)** a continuous protective detail.\n\nAn assessment may specify a level in part, may combine measures from more than one level, and may specify a measure not enumerated where the risk found requires it.\n\n**(b-1A) A finding is not a prediction, and nothing here authorizes a score.** A finding under this Section is a judgment about **what protective measures the situation warrants**, and is **not a prediction that any person will act**. **Nothing in this Section authorizes the creation of a score, a rating, a profile, a predictive instrument, or any list, status, or designation that attaches to a person who is the subject of an assessment**, and no such score, profile, or status may be created, maintained, or relied upon in performing an assessment. An assessment rests on the conduct and circumstances found, stated in the record of the assessment.\n\n**(b-2) The lowest level that meets the risk, and nothing by reason of office.** An assessment shall specify **the lowest level that meets the risk it finds**, and shall state the facts on which that level rests. **No level of protection attaches to any person by reason of the office held, the court on which the person sits, the seniority of the person, or membership in any class.** A level is specified only on facts concerning the risk to the particular person, found by the professional staff performing the assessment.\n\n**(b-3) Escalation and de-escalation on facts, at any time.** An assessment may be performed at any time on information indicating that the risk to a person has changed, on the request of the person, on referral, or on the initiative of the performing service; and the level shall be raised or lowered to the lowest level that meets the risk then found. **A change in level requires a change in the facts and not merely the passage of time**, and \u00a7 10A-4(e) governs the reasons for which a level may not be lowered.\n\n**(c) It may be declined.** A person entitled to protection under subsection (b) may decline it, in writing, in whole or in part, at any time. A declination is not a finding, does not affect any later assessment, and may be withdrawn.\n\n**(d) Continuation.** Protection provided under subsection (b) continues while the finding stands, and may be discontinued or reduced **only** upon a subsequent assessment, performed under the same published criteria, finding that the threat has abated or diminished. The subsequent assessment shall be in writing and shall be recorded.\n\n**(e) Not for a reason relating to the exercise of judicial power.** Protection shall not be discontinued, reduced, varied, or delayed by reason of a judicial officer's decision, opinion, vote, recusal, or public statement, by reason of any matter before or decided by the officer, by reason of the identity of any party to such a matter, **or by reason of any other consideration bearing on how the officer has exercised or may exercise judicial power.** The enumeration in this subsection is illustrative of the general prohibition its final clause states, and **the omission of any consideration from it is a determination of Congress and not a gap.** **An action taken for such a reason is void, and the protection continues as though the action had not been taken.**\n\n**(f) Persons who have left judicial office.** A person who has ceased to hold judicial office is eligible for assessment and protection under this Section where the threat assessed arises from duties performed in that office, on the same terms as a person in office.\n\n**(f-1) Enforcement by the person entitled.** The duties under subsections (a), (a-1), (a-2), (b) and (d), and under \u00a7 10A-1(b-2)(4), are **ministerial**, and a person entitled to an assessment or to protection under this Section **may bring a civil action in a district court of the United States to compel it**, and is entitled to costs and reasonable attorney's fees on prevailing. The action is on the plaintiff's own entitlement, is not brought on behalf of the United States, assigns no claim of the United States, and **does not require the participation, consent, or non-opposition of the Attorney General or of any other officer.** The record of the assessment, and of any determination under subsection (d), shall be furnished to the plaintiff, subject to such protective order as the court enters.\n\n**(g) Aggregate publication.** The Administrative Office shall publish annually the number of assessments performed, the number performed under subsection (a-1), the number finding a threat, the number in which protection was provided, declined, discontinued, or reduced, and the number of discontinuations following a subsequent assessment under subsection (d), without identifying any person, any court, or any matter."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-4a","heading":"\u00a7 10A-4A. Measures Available Without an Assessment","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) Available on request, to anyone covered.** The following are available **on request, without a threat assessment, without a reported threat, and without any showing**, to every judicial officer, every judicial employee, and every member of the household of either:\n\n**(1)** a **residential security survey** \u2014 an inspection of the person's residence and the publication to that person of what it finds and what it recommends;\n\n**(2)** **personal security training**, for the person and for members of the household, including the training described in section 5935 of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022; and\n\n**(3)** **suppression of covered information** under \u00a7 10A-5 and under the Personal Privacy Act, and enrollment in any mechanism either provides.\n\n**(a-1) Who provides them, in what form, and by when.** The **Administrative Office** shall provide the measures in subsection (a), directly or by procurement, within the period set in the schedule to this Act after a request. **A survey under subsection (a)(1) is satisfied in the first instance by a self-administered instrument the Administrative Office publishes, together with a review of the completed instrument by a person qualified to conduct a security survey**; an **on-site inspection** shall be provided on the request of the person, or where that review identifies a condition warranting one. Training under subsection (a)(2) may be provided in group, recorded, or written form. **It shall be offered at the interval set in the schedule to this Act and not only on appointment or entry on duty**; each offer and each completion shall be recorded; and the Administrative Office shall publish annually **the proportion of persons offered the training who completed it, by district.** **The absence of a published instrument does not suspend subsection (a)**; until one is published, a survey is provided by on-site inspection.\n\n**(b) A request is not a finding, and carries no consequence.** A request under subsection (a) is not a threat report, is not an assessment, and **is not evidence that any risk exists**. It shall not be recorded in, or reported to, any body other than the office performing the measure; and **no adverse inference, and no consequence of any kind, attaches to making one or to declining to make one.**\n\n**(c) Not a substitute for an assessment.** A measure under this Section neither satisfies nor discharges any duty under \u00a7 10A-4, and providing one is not a ground for declining or deferring an assessment.\n\n**(e) The program reports what it learns.** The Administrative Office shall publish annually, in aggregate and without identifying any person, court, or location: the number of surveys, trainings, and suppression enrollments provided under subsection (a); the proportion of recommendations from surveys under subsection (a)(1) that were subsequently implemented, and the cost of implementing them; and, for each of the measures in subsection (a), **the number of persons who received it who were thereafter the subject of an assessment finding a threat, and the number who were not.**\n\n**(f) Because the record does not exist.** No published evaluation establishes whether the measures in subsection (a) reduce threats or attacks against public officials, in either direction. Subsection (e) is drafted so that **this program generates that record rather than assuming it**, and the Administrative Office shall publish the data in a form permitting a person outside the Government to analyze it."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-4b","heading":"\u00a7 10A-4B. Cases, and What Must Become of Them","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) The duty to open a case and to reach a disposition.** Upon a report of concerning behavior received through the channel under \u00a7 10A-4(a-4), or upon concerning behavior otherwise coming to its attention, the United States Marshals Service **shall open a case where the behavior is conduct subsection (c-2) describes, assess it, and reach a disposition under subsection (b) within the period set in the schedule to this Act.** **A case is not disposed of by inaction**, and the expiration of that period without a recorded disposition is itself reportable under subsection (f). **Behavior that, judged on the report and on information the Marshals Service lawfully holds, is not conduct subsection (c-2) describes opens no case.** For each report or other behavior that opens no case, the Marshals Service shall record, within the period this subsection sets, the determination that it opens no case, with its date and the facts on which it rests, stating what was assessed and why it is not such conduct. A report that opens no case remains a report for purposes of \u00a7 10A-4, including subsection (a-2) of that Section, and subsection (d) governs the information in it, and any information obtained in making the determination, as it governs information in a case closed under subsection (b)(1), the retention period running from the earlier of the determination and the end of the period this subsection sets.\n\n**(b) The dispositions.** A case is disposed of by one of the following, which shall be recorded with its date and the facts on which it rests \u2014\n\n**(1)** **closure**, upon a finding that the conduct does not warrant further action;\n\n**(2)** **monitoring** for a stated period, subject to subsection (e);\n\n**(3)** **contact** with the person, by the Service or by a State or local agency acting with it;\n\n**(4)** **referral** to a mental-health, social-service, or veterans' authority, on the terms that authority's law provides;\n\n**(5)** **referral** to a United States Attorney or to a State or local prosecutor; or\n\n**(6)** **referral** to a court for a proceeding a statute provides.\n\n**(b-1) What does not satisfy subsection (b).** A record stating only the category of disposition, or stating a conclusion without the facts on which it rests, **does not satisfy subsection (b)**, and the case is treated as not disposed of. A disposition under subsection (b)(1) shall state what was assessed and why it does not warrant further action.\n\n**(c) A case, and no apparatus.** This Section authorizes the opening of a case **upon a report or upon conduct that has come to the Service's attention, and only as subsections (a) and (c-2) provide, and authorizes no program of monitoring, scanning, collection, or analysis directed at persons who are not the subject of a case.** **The omission from this Act of any such authority is a determination of Congress and not a gap**, and section 5936(b)(1) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 is **not** converted into a duty by \u00a7 10A-7 or by any other provision of this Act.\n\n**(c-1) What may not open a case, select a disposition, or make a referral.** No case may be opened, no disposition under subsection (b) selected, and no referral made, on the basis of a person's speech on a matter of public concern, association, political affiliation, religion, national origin, or petition to a government, **except upon conduct subsection (c-2) describes**; and the fact that a person's grievance concerns a court, a judge, or a decision is not itself concerning behavior.\n\n**(c-2) A case acts on conduct, and not on what a grievance says.** A case may be opened, a disposition other than closure under subsection (b)(1) selected, and monitoring renewed under subsection (e), **only on communication or conduct that, beyond the content of any grievance, opinion, or criticism it expresses, indicates an interest in, a capability for, an intent toward, or preparation for physical harm to a judicial officer, a judicial employee, or a member of the household of either, or damage to a place at which any of them works or resides.** **The content of a grievance, opinion, or criticism, however persistent, repeated, or intemperate its expression, is not such conduct**, and neither is the number of times a person communicates about a matter. **A threat of such harm or damage is such conduct, whatever grievance, opinion, or criticism accompanies it.** Where the assessment of a case finds no such conduct, the case is closed under subsection (b)(1). **This subsection governs every case this Section authorizes, and does not narrow \u00a7 10A-0(d-1) as it applies to \u00a7 10A-4, including subsection (a-2) of that Section, or to a report through the channel under \u00a7 10A-4(a-4).**\n\n**(d) Closed use-list, and deletion.** Information in a case may be used **only** to assess and dispose of that case, to protect a person under \u00a7 10A-4, and to make a referral under subsection (b). It **may not** be disseminated for any other purpose, matched against any other database except as a disposition under subsection (b) requires, or retained beyond the period set in the schedule to this Act following disposition \u2014 **after which it is deleted and the deletion certified.** Where a case is closed under subsection (b)(1), the retention period is the shorter period the schedule sets.\n\n**(e) Monitoring is bounded, is defined, and is not a status.** **\"Monitoring\" means the periodic review of information the Marshals Service already lawfully holds and of any further report concerning the person. It does not authorize physical surveillance, the interception of any communication, the acquisition of any record that a statute makes available only on process, the acquisition of any record by purchase, or the use of any covert technique**, and none of those is authorized by this Section. A disposition of monitoring runs for a stated period not exceeding the period set in the schedule to this Act, rests on a written statement of the conduct subsection (c-2) describes that warrants it, and may be renewed **only on a new written statement of such conduct occurring during the period.** Monitoring creates no list, no designation, and no status, and \u00a7 10A-4(b-1A) applies to it.\n\n**(f) What the program publishes.** The Marshals Service shall publish annually, in aggregate and without identifying any person: cases opened and the source of the report; reports and other behavior that opened no case under subsection (a), by source; dispositions by category under subsection (b); time to disposition, including cases exceeding the period under subsection (a); and **the number of cases in which a person who had been the subject of a case thereafter engaged in violence against a person this Act protects.**\n\n**(g) Being a subject is not a finding.** That a person is or was the subject of a case **is not a finding of wrongdoing**, and may not be used to deny or condition any right, benefit, license, employment, or security clearance.\n\n**(h) Used against a person only if disclosed to them.** Information in a case **may not be used against the person in any proceeding, or as a basis for any adverse action against the person, unless it is disclosed to that person** and the person is afforded an opportunity to respond. This subsection does not require notice to a person that a case exists or has existed."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-5","heading":"\u00a7 10A-5. Removal of Covered Information, and a Remedy That Runs to the Person","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) The action lies with the person.** Section 5934(f)(1) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 (28 U.S.C. \u00a7 601 note) is amended by striking *\"the Director of the Administrative Office of the United States Courts, or the designee of the Director, may file an action seeking injunctive or declaratory relief in any court of competent jurisdiction, through the Department of Justice\"* and inserting **\"the at-risk individual, or the Director of the Administrative Office of the United States Courts or the designee of the Director, may file an action seeking injunctive or declaratory relief in any court of competent jurisdiction. An action under this paragraph is not conducted by, and does not require the participation or consent of, the Department of Justice or any officer of the executive branch.\"**\n\n**(a-1) The chief judge's authority is unaffected.** Section 5934(f)(2) of that Act confers on the respective chief judge the same authority as the Director under section 5934(f)(1), and that authority extends to section 5934(f)(1) as amended by subsection (a). **The omission of the chief judge from the text inserted by subsection (a) is not a limitation on section 5934(f)(2)**, which continues to operate.\n\n**(b) The ceiling on a government agency is struck, and the amount runs to the person harmed.** Section 5934(f)(3)(A) of that Act is amended by striking clause (i) and inserting **\"(i) award to the at-risk individual or their immediate family, as applicable, an amount the court determines to compensate for the disclosure, having regard to its duration, the number of persons to whom the covered information was made available, and any protective measure the disclosure made necessary; and\"**. **The amount is compensatory, is paid to the person harmed rather than to the Treasury, is not imposed for unlawful conduct as such, and is not a fine or penalty.**\n\n**(c) Removal is ministerial.** The duty of a government agency under section 5934(a)(2) of that Act to remove covered information within 72 hours of a written request is a ministerial duty, to which no discretion attaches, **enforceable by mandamus at the suit of the at-risk individual or of any person the individual authorizes.**\n\n**(d) Fees.** A court shall award costs and reasonable attorney's fees to an at-risk individual who prevails in an action under subsection (a) or (c). **This award is in addition to any award under section 5934(f)(3) of that Act**, which is available only upon a knowing violation of an order already granted; the omission from this subsection of that paragraph's conditions is a determination of Congress and not a gap.\n\n**(e) Time to sue.** An action under section 5934(f)(1) of that Act, or under subsection (c), shall be brought within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the at-risk individual knew or reasonably should have known of the publication or of the failure to remove. **A continuing publication is a continuing violation, and the period runs from its last day.**\n\n**(f) Cumulative.** This Section is in addition to every remedy available under that Act, under the Personal Privacy Act, and under any other law, and displaces none of them."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-6","heading":"\u00a7 10A-6. The Judiciary's Own Security Record","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"Section 5936(c)(3) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 (28 U.S.C. \u00a7 601 note) is amended by striking *\"the Attorney General determines\"* and inserting **\"the Judicial Conference of the United States determines\"**. The determination whether publication of a report on the security of the judiciary would endanger a judicial officer is made within the judicial branch."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-7","heading":"\u00a7 10A-7. Authorities Made Duties","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"Section 5936(a)(1) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 (28 U.S.C. \u00a7 601 note) is amended by striking *\"The Federal judiciary is authorized to perform\"* and inserting **\"The Federal judiciary shall perform\"**. The functions enumerated in subparagraphs (A) through (D) of that paragraph are performed as this Act provides and are funded under \u00a7 10A-9."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-8","heading":"\u00a7 10A-8. Transfer of Covered Information to a Government","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"Section 5937(a)(4) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022 (28 U.S.C. \u00a7 601 note), which provides that nothing in that subtitle is construed to prohibit information sharing by a data broker to a Federal, State, Tribal, or local government, **is a rule of construction governing that subtitle and nothing else. It authorizes no transfer that \u00a7 4-3(c) of the Personal Privacy Act, section 2714 of title 18, United States Code, or any other law prohibits**, and the omission from that paragraph of the limits those provisions impose is not a determination that they do not apply."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-9","heading":"\u00a7 10A-9. Resources for Judicial Security","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) Authorization.** There are authorized to be appropriated to the Administrative Office of the United States Courts, for each fiscal year, such sums as are necessary to carry out this Section, including the assessments under \u00a7 10A-4, the measures made available by \u00a7 10A-4A, the cases required by \u00a7 10A-4B, the protection required by \u00a7 10A-4(b), the procurement authorized by \u00a7 10A-2 and \u00a7 10A-3(b), and the functions made duties by \u00a7 10A-7. Amounts appropriated under this subsection are available until expended.\n\n**(b) Permanent appropriation.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for **the assessments required by \u00a7 10A-4(a) and (a-1), the measures made available by \u00a7 10A-4A(a), the opening, assessment and disposition of cases, and the determinations, required by \u00a7 10A-4B(a), the protection required by \u00a7 10A-4(b) and its continuation under \u00a7 10A-4(d), the removal of covered information under \u00a7 10A-5(c), and the procurement of protection by the Administrative Office under \u00a7 10A-3(b), to the extent the protection procured is protection an assessment under \u00a7 10A-4 has found to be required**. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse.\n\n**(c) Continuity during a lapse.** The assessments required by \u00a7 10A-4, the protection required by \u00a7 10A-4(b), and the removal of covered information under \u00a7 10A-5(c) are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, and obligations incurred for them are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code. The officers and employees necessary to perform them shall be designated as excepted from furlough, and the Administrative Office shall identify those positions in the judiciary's shutdown plan and publish the identification.\n\n**(d) No displacement.** Amounts appropriated under this Section are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to the judiciary or to the United States Marshals Service. **No reduction in an appropriation to the Marshals Service is authorized by reason of this Section**, and the procurement authority of \u00a7 10A-3(b) is not a substitute for the appropriation of amounts necessary for the Service to perform its duty under \u00a7 10A-3(a)."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-9a","heading":"\u00a7 10A-9A. Tax Treatment of Protection Provided","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) Not income.** Protection provided under this Section, and security equipment and its installation, monitoring, maintenance, and removal provided under \u00a7 10A-2 or \u00a7 10A-4, are **not gross income** to the person protected or to any member of that person's household, are not wages for purposes of chapter 21, 22, 23, or 24 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and are not a gift for purposes of chapter 12 of that Code.\n\n**(b) Without regard to the fringe-benefit conditions, and without a determination by any officer.** Subsection (a) applies **without regard to** whether a bona fide business-oriented security concern exists within the meaning of section 1.132-5(m) of title 26, Code of Federal Regulations, whether an overall security program has been established, and whether protection is provided on a 24-hour basis; and **without any determination by the Secretary of the Treasury, the Commissioner of Internal Revenue, or any other officer.** A determination under \u00a7 10A-1 or an assessment under \u00a7 10A-4 that protection is required is conclusive for purposes of this Section.\n\n**(c) Title to equipment.** Whether title to equipment installed under this Section remains in the United States or passes to the person protected does not affect subsection (a), and no amount is includible in gross income upon the installation, use, retention, removal, or transfer of the equipment, or upon the person ceasing to hold judicial office.\n\n**(d) Costs and fees.** An amount awarded as costs or attorney's fees under \u00a7 10A-1(b-2)(6) or \u00a7 10A-5(d), or under section 5934(f)(3) of the Daniel Anderl Judicial Security and Privacy Act of 2022, **is excluded from the gross income of the person to whom it is awarded to the extent it is paid to counsel**, whether paid directly to counsel or to the individual.\n\n**(e) No inference.** Nothing in this Section determines the treatment of protection or security equipment provided to any person under any other authority, and the enumeration in subsection (b) of conditions disregarded is not a determination that any of them would otherwise apply."},{"anchor":"sec-10a-10","heading":"\u00a7 10A-10. Construction and Severability of This Section","part":"SECTION 10A \u2014 JUDICIAL SECURITY","body":"**(a) Independence.** Nothing in this Section confers authority on any officer of the United States outside the judicial branch to review, approve, or act upon a determination, an assessment, or a decision of a judicial officer, and nothing in it conditions the exercise of the judicial power. This Section is applied together with \u00a7 11-2A.\n\n**(b) Floor, not ceiling.** This Section reduces no protection available under existing law, and where existing law affords greater protection it continues to apply.\n\n**(c) Severability.** Each provision of this Section is severable from every other and from every other provision of this Act. **If \u00a7 10A-1 is held invalid, \u00a7\u00a7 10A-2 through 10A-9 remain in force**, the Administrative Office's procurement authority and the assessment-and-protection requirement operating without regard to which body determines the branch's security requirements. If \u00a7 10A-4(b) is held invalid, the assessment and publication requirements of \u00a7 10A-4 remain in force. **The action under \u00a7 10A-1(b-2)(6) is severable from the duties it enforces; if it is held invalid, or held not to present a case or controversy as to any plaintiff, the withholding and publication duties of \u00a7 10A-1(b-2) remain in force.** **If \u00a7 10A-2A(a) is held invalid, \u00a7 10A-2A(f) operates and every other provision of this Section remains in force**; the protection this Section requires is then performed by persons deputized as \u00a7 10A-2A(f) directs."},{"anchor":"sec-11-1","heading":"\u00a7 11-1. Relationship to Existing Law","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Floor, not ceiling.** No provision of this Act reduces, repeals, or supersedes any obligation of existing law, including the Ethics in Government Act, 28 U.S.C. \u00a7 455, the Judicial Conduct and Disability Act, and the requirements of the Courthouse Ethics and Transparency Act. Where existing law is stricter, it continues to apply; where this Act is stricter, it governs.\n\n**(b) Method of amendment.** The provisions of this Act that extend or supplement existing law are to be executed, in final drafting, as amendments to title 28 of the United States Code and to the Ethics in Government Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-11-2","heading":"\u00a7 11-2. Preservation of Judicial Independence","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Nothing in this Act shall be construed to impair the independence of the judiciary, to condition the exercise of the judicial power, to affect the assignment of any matter, or to influence the decision of any case.\n\n**(b)** No obligation under this Act operates to prevent a covered judge from performing the duties of the office. Where an obligation would have that effect, the judge shall recuse or divest as the applicable rule provides, and the duties of the office are performed.\n\n**(c)** No provision of this Act shall be applied so as to diminish the compensation of a covered judge. If any application of this Act would have that effect, that application is void and the remainder of the Act continues in force."},{"anchor":"sec-11-2a","heading":"\u00a7 11-2A. No Supervision of Judicial Decisionmaking; Independence of Each Obligation","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) No scheme of supervision.** This Act establishes no scheme of legislative supervision over the judiciary, confers no authority on the Congress or on any officer of the United States to direct, review, or influence the decision of any matter, and creates no relationship of oversight between the Congress and any court. Each obligation imposed by this Act is a discrete requirement of disclosure, conduct, administration, or employment.\n\n**(b) Each obligation freestanding.** Each obligation imposed by this Act stands independently of every other. No obligation is a component of, conditioned upon, or given meaning by any other, and the operation of each is complete in itself. Congress would have enacted each obligation of this Act standing alone, in the absence of every other, and directs that each be so construed and, if necessary, so preserved.\n\n**(c) Construction against aggregation.** No provision of this Act shall be construed as part of a larger scheme by reason of its enactment in the same statute as any other provision. A holding that any provision or group of provisions is invalid shall not be extended to any other provision on the ground that the provisions together constitute a scheme, a design, or a pattern.\n\n**(d) Rules already adopted.** Where this Act requires a court or the Judicial Conference to adopt, publish, or follow a rule, standard, or procedure, a rule, standard, or procedure already adopted and published that addresses the subjects this Act enumerates satisfies the requirement. Nothing in this Act displaces a measure the judiciary has adopted or may adopt of its own motion, and the judiciary may at any time satisfy a requirement of this Act by acting under its own authority."},{"anchor":"sec-11-2b","heading":"\u00a7 11-2B. Requirements of Judicial Rulemaking","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Subjects, not content.** Where this Act requires the adoption of a rule, standard, or procedure, it prescribes the subjects the measure shall address and does not prescribe how any subject is resolved. The choice among permissible resolutions belongs to the court or body adopting the measure.\n\n**(b) Minimum requirements of form.** A measure adopted under such a requirement shall: address each subject this Act enumerates; be published in a public, machine-readable form; state the date of adoption and of each amendment; and be applied on the record in each instance to which it pertains, with the record identifying the measure applied.\n\n**(c) Compliance reported.** The annual report under \u00a7 4-5 shall state, for each requirement of judicial rulemaking in this Act, whether a measure has been adopted, whether it addresses each enumerated subject, the date of adoption, and where it is published. Where no measure has been adopted, the report shall so state.\n\n**(d) Purpose.** Subsections (b) and (c) exist so that a requirement of this Act is not satisfied by a measure that addresses nothing. They set requirements of form and publication. They do not prescribe the substance of any measure, and no measure is invalid, and no obligation unsatisfied, by reason of the content the adopting authority chooses."},{"anchor":"sec-11-2c","heading":"\u00a7 11-2C. Independence From the Other Acts","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"No provision of this Act depends for its operation or validity on the enactment or continued effect of the People's Prosperity Act, the Executive Integrity Act, the Congressional Integrity Act, or the Candidate Integrity Act. Where this Act refers to a fund or instrument established by another Act and that Act is not in effect, the eligible assets available under \u00a7 0-3(c) are those remaining, and the election under Section 2 operates without regard to the unavailable option."},{"anchor":"sec-11-2d","heading":"\u00a7 11-2D. Self-Executing Obligations","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Obligations that operate without any determination.** The following operate without any determination, election, or action by any officer of the United States or of any court, and no officer, court, or body may suspend, waive, or decline to give them effect: the duty of a covered judge to file under \u00a7 1-1 and \u00a7 1-2; the duty of a custodian to report under \u00a7 1-2(b); the operation of the conflict-screening system under \u00a7 1-3 and the transmission of the notices it generates; the publication of filings, elections, and recusals in the database under Section 3; the disclosure obligations of Section 6; the prohibitions of \u00a7 6-10 and \u00a7 6-11; the reporting obligations of Section 10; the assessments required by \u00a7 10A-4(a) and (a-1) and the protection required by \u00a7 10A-4(b) and continued by \u00a7 10A-4(d); and the removal of covered information made ministerial by \u00a7 10A-5(c).\n\n**(b) Publication is ministerial.** The publication required by Section 3 and by \u00a7 1-2(a) is a ministerial duty. No discretion attaches to it, and it is enforceable by mandamus at the suit of any person. A redaction under \u00a7 3-5 is the only permissible departure, is limited to what that Section allows, and does not relieve the obligation to publish the remainder.\n\n**(c) Inaction does not excuse.** The failure of the Administrative Office, the Judicial Conference, a judicial council, or any court to act does not excuse, toll, or diminish an obligation under subsection (a), and compliance with any such obligation is not conditioned on any authority having acted.\n\n**(d) Not a ground for disqualification or relief in a matter.** Nothing in this Section creates a ground for disqualification, supplies a basis for vacating or reopening any decision, or affords any remedy in a matter before a covered judge. Its remedies are confined to compelling the filing, screening, or publication required by this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-11-3","heading":"\u00a7 11-3. Effective Date","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Sections 1 and 3 take effect one year after enactment, to permit the Administrative Office to establish the systems required.\n\n**(b)** Section 2 takes effect upon enactment. An election may be made at any time thereafter.\n\n**(c)** Sections \u00a7 5-0, \u00a7 5-A, and \u00a7 5-6 take effect upon enactment, and the first transition under \u00a7 5-6(c) occurs as that provision provides. Sections \u00a7 5-1 through \u00a7 5-5 take effect upon enactment to the extent they are within the power of Congress, and the first opening under \u00a7 5-3 occurs as that provision provides.\n\n**(d)** Sections 6, 7, 8, and 10 take effect one year after enactment.\n\n**(e)** Section 9 takes effect one year after enactment, except that **\u00a7 9-4, protecting a reporting employee from retaliation, and \u00a7 9-5, voiding provisions that bar reporting, take effect upon enactment**, and \u00a7 9-5 applies to any agreement whenever made.\n\n**(f)** Section 10A takes effect upon enactment, except that \u00a7 10A-4 takes effect upon the publication of the criteria required by \u00a7 10A-4(a) or one year after enactment, whichever is earlier. **Where the criteria have not been published on the date \u00a7 10A-4 takes effect, an assessment under that Section is performed on the criteria the United States Marshals Service then applies in practice, which the Administrative Office shall publish, withholding nothing except as \u00a7 10A-1(b-2) provides; the absence of published criteria does not suspend \u00a7 10A-4.**"},{"anchor":"sec-11-3a","heading":"\u00a7 11-3A. Amendment of Core Provisions","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Entrenchment.** An amendment to the disclosure and conflict-screening requirements of Section 1, or to the voluntary election of Section 2, requires a two-thirds vote of each House, a recorded roll-call vote, and an effective date no earlier than three years after enactment of the amendment.\n\n**(b) Basis and limits.** The vote and roll-call requirements of subsection (a) are enacted as an exercise of the rulemaking power of each House of Congress, operate as rules of the proceedings of each House, and may be changed by either House in the same manner and to the same extent as any other rule of its proceedings. A court that declines to treat this subsection as binding leaves Sections 1 and 2 in force as ordinary law, unaffected by that holding, unless and until repealed by whatever vote is otherwise sufficient. This subsection does not purport to bind any future Congress beyond what Article I permits, and does not condition, coerce, or penalize any judge's election under Section 2 in any way."},{"anchor":"sec-11-4","heading":"\u00a7 11-4. Severability","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder of this Act and its application to other persons or circumstances are not affected. This Section is to be applied together with \u00a7 11-2A, which states that each obligation of this Act is freestanding and that no provision is to be construed as part of a larger scheme by reason of its enactment alongside any other. In particular:\n\n**(a)** The disclosure and transaction-reporting provisions of Section 1, the conflict-screening provisions of \u00a7 1-3, the voluntary election of Section 2, the database of Section 3, and the enforcement provisions of Section 4 are each independently severable. If the voluntary election of Section 2 is held invalid, the disclosure, screening, and database provisions remain in force and continue to operate.\n\n**(b)** The application of this Act to Justices of the Supreme Court is severable from its application to other covered judges, so that a holding as to one does not affect the other.\n\n**(c)** The application of this Act to a judge who takes office after enactment is severable from its application to a judge in service on the date of enactment.\n\n**(d)** If any provision is held to diminish judicial compensation, to affect judicial tenure, or to vest authority over the judiciary in the executive branch, that provision is severed and the remainder of this Act continues in force.\n\n**(d-1) Each disclosure provision severable and independently justified.** The disclosure obligations of \u00a7 1-1, \u00a7 1-2, \u00a7 6-1 through \u00a7 6-8, \u00a7 10-1, and \u00a7 10-2 are each severable from every other, and each rests on its own justification. No such provision is offered in support of any other, and a holding that one is invalid shall not be extended to any other by reason of a shared subject matter. Within \u00a7 10-1, subsection (a), the purpose-or-direction obligation of subsection (a-1), and the common-funding obligation of subsection (a-2) are each severable from the others.\n\n**(d-2) Self-executing rule severable.** Section \u00a7 11-2D is severable from every other provision. If the mandamus remedy of \u00a7 11-2D(b) is held unavailable, the obligations of subsection (a) continue and are enforced as this Act otherwise provides; and if any obligation in subsection (a) is held invalid, the remainder of that subsection is unaffected.\n\n**(e) Each new obligation severable.** Section 6 (gifts, travel, hospitality, outside income, wagers, and non-public information), Section 7 (recusal and case assignment), Section 8 (code of conduct and Supreme Court transparency), Section 9 (workplace protections), Section 10 (amicus and nomination transparency), and Section 10A (judicial security) are each severable from one another and from every other Section, and each Section of each is severable from every other Section of it.\n\n**(f) Supreme Court applications severable.** Within Sections 6, 7, and 8, the application of any provision to a Justice of the Supreme Court is severable from its application to any other covered judge. If a provision is held invalid as applied to a Justice, it remains in force as to every other covered judge.\n\n**(g) Review panel severable.** If the review panel of \u00a7 8-2 is held invalid, \u00a7 8-1 remains in force and the Supreme Court's obligation to adopt a code with a resolution mechanism under \u00a7 8-1(c) is unaffected.\n\n**(h) Workplace protections severable by statute and by remedy.** Within \u00a7 9-1, the application of each enumerated statute is severable from the application of every other, and within Section 9 the substantive protections are severable from the forum provision of \u00a7 9-2 and from the Office established by \u00a7 9-3. If the out-of-circuit forum of \u00a7 9-2 is held invalid, an action under \u00a7 9-1 proceeds under the ordinary rules of venue and assignment.\n\n**(i) Disclosure severable from prohibition.** Within Section 6, the reporting obligations are severable from the prohibitions of \u00a7 6-9, \u00a7 6-10, and \u00a7 6-11, each prohibition is severable from the others, and if a prohibition is held invalid the reporting obligations remain."},{"anchor":"sec-11-5","heading":"\u00a7 11-5. Resources and Continuity of Operations","part":"SECTION 11 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Authorization.** There are authorized to be appropriated to the Administrative Office of the United States Courts, to the Judicial Conference, and to the Office of Judicial Workplace Integrity, for each fiscal year, such sums as are necessary to carry out this Act, including: the database required by Section 3; the automated conflict-screening system required by \u00a7 1-3; the custodial reporting and auto-population required by \u00a7 1-2(b); the free filing tools required by \u00a7 4-3; the advisory opinion function under \u00a7 4-2A, including staff to prepare opinions and maintain the shared library; the electronic public access required by \u00a7 8-6, including the amounts necessary to replace the fees eliminated by that Section; the Office established by \u00a7 9-3; the training required by \u00a7 9-6 and the surveys required by \u00a7 9-7; and the additional judgeships authorized by \u00a7 5-6(b-1), including chambers, staff, and facilities.\n\n**(b) No-year availability.** Amounts appropriated under subsection (a) are available until expended.\n\n**(b-1) Permanent appropriation for the self-executing core.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for **the conflict-screening system required by \u00a7 1-3**, for the receipt and preservation of the filings required by this Act, and for the publication this Act makes ministerial. Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse. **The obligations \u00a7 11-2D declares self-executing, the publication \u00a7 11-2D(b) makes ministerial, and the screening that prevents a judge from sitting on a matter in which the judge holds an interest, do not depend on an annual appropriation.**\n\n**(b-2) The permanent core reaches the functions a person waits on.** The appropriation made by subsection (b-1) extends to **the issuance of opinions under \u00a7 4-2A, the decision of applicability requests, the disposition of complaints, the issuance of certificates under \u00a7 11-2D, and the preparation and filing of reports on behalf of a covered person where this Act provides for it** \u2014 and **covers the personnel who perform them**, including opinion counsel, valuation staff and filing-assistance staff. **Subsection (c) therefore does not reach these functions**, and the priority order in subsection (d) has nothing to rank as to them.\n\n**(c) No obligation without appropriation.** **Except for the duties funded by subsection (b-1), which are effective in every fiscal year without regard to this subsection**, no duty imposed by this Act is effective in a fiscal year except to the extent that amounts are appropriated to carry it out. Where amounts are insufficient, the Administrative Office shall publish a statement identifying each duty not performed and the amount required, and shall transmit it to the Congress. No penalty or consequence attaches to any court, officer, or employee for a duty not performed for want of appropriation.\n\n**(c-1) This section does not qualify \u00a7 11-2D.** No obligation described in \u00a7 11-2D, no duty this Act makes ministerial, and no duty funded by subsection (b-1) is excused, tolled, suspended, or rendered ineffective by the absence or insufficiency of an appropriation, by a failure to request one, or by a failure to apportion, allot, or obligate amounts appropriated. **A defense that the duty was not effective, or was excused, for want of funds is not available in any action to enforce such an obligation or duty.**\n\n**(d) Order of priority.** Where amounts are insufficient, duties shall be performed in the following order: first, the conflict-screening system under \u00a7 1-3, which prevents participation in a matter in which a judge holds an interest; second, the receipt and preservation of filings; third, publication in the database under Section 3; fourth, the intake and referral functions of the Office under \u00a7 9-3; fifth, the issuance of advisory opinions under \u00a7 4-2A; and sixth, every remaining duty.\n\n**(e) No displacement.** Amounts appropriated under this Act are in addition to, and shall not be derived from, amounts otherwise available to the judiciary. No court or judicial-branch agency shall reduce, delay, or discontinue any function it performed before enactment in order to carry out this Act. The additional judgeships authorized by \u00a7 5-6(b-1) shall not be funded by reducing the resources of existing judgeships.\n\n**(f) Annual certification of adequacy.** The Administrative Office shall certify annually whether appropriated amounts are sufficient, and shall state, where they are not, the amount required and the consequence of the shortfall. The certification is published and transmitted to the Congress.\n\n**(g) Continuity during a lapse in appropriations.** The following are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, and obligations incurred for them are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code: the operation of the conflict-screening system under \u00a7 1-3 and the transmission of notices it generates; the receipt of filings and custodial reports under Section 1; the preservation of those records; publication in the database under Section 3; the receipt of complaints by the Office under \u00a7 9-3 and the protection of the identity of the persons who make them; and electronic public access to court records under \u00a7 8-6. **The activities funded by subsection (b-2) are within this subsection**, and the personnel who perform them are within subsection (h).\n\n**(h) Excepted personnel.** The officers and employees necessary to perform the activities in subsection (g) shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations. The Administrative Office shall identify those positions in the judiciary's shutdown plan, publish the identification, and maintain staffing sufficient to perform the activities without interruption.\n\n**(i) Deadlines.** A deadline imposed by this Act on a covered judge or on any other person is tolled for the duration of a lapse in appropriations affecting the Administrative Office, and for 14 days thereafter. A deadline is not tolled as to the activities in subsection (g)."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Transaction reporting de minimis threshold** (\u00a7 1-2(a)): [$___]\n2. **Appropriation to the Administrative Office for the database and conflict-screening system** (\u00a7\u00a7 1-3, 3-1): [such sums as necessary]\n3. **Appropriation for the additional judgeships authorized under \u00a7 5-6(b-1)**, including chambers, staff, and facilities: [such sums as necessary]\n5A. **Appropriation for chambers, law clerks, and staff of a Senior Justice sitting under \u00a7 5-A(f)**: [such sums as necessary, on the scale provided to a judge in regular active service on the court of assignment]\n4. **Gift reporting threshold** (\u00a7 6-1(a)): [$___, indexed]\n5. **Amicus contributor disclosure threshold** (\u00a7 10-1(a)): [$___, indexed]\n6. **Nomination-advocacy disclosure threshold** (\u00a7 10-2(a)): [$___, indexed]\n7. **Appropriation for the Office of Judicial Workplace Integrity** (\u00a7 9-3): [such sums as necessary]\n8. **Appropriation to replace electronic public access fees** (\u00a7 8-6): [such sums as necessary]\n9. **Appropriation for the advisory opinion function** (\u00a7 4-2A): [such sums as necessary, including staff to prepare opinions, maintain the shared library, and meet the deadlines and staffing-adequacy requirements of \u00a7 4-2A(c) and (m)]\n10. **Period within which the Administrative Office shall furnish a filing on request while the database lacks an entry** (\u00a7 3-1(b)): [___ days]\n11. **Period for the United States to publish an emergency application and its supplements** (\u00a7 8-4B(b)): [___ days after filing]\n12. **Period within which the Marshals Service shall supply a published security requirement** (\u00a7 10A-3(a)): [___ days after publication]\n13. **Period within which the Marshals Service shall publish a statement of a requirement not supplied** (\u00a7 10A-3(b)): [___ days]\n14. **Period within which a threat assessment shall be performed on request, referral, or initiative** (\u00a7 10A-4(a)): [___ days]\n15. **Interval at which a threat assessment shall be performed as to each judicial officer** (\u00a7 10A-4(a)): [___ months]\n16. **Appropriation for judicial security** (\u00a7 10A-9): [such sums as necessary, including assessments, protection, procurement, and the functions made duties by \u00a7 10A-7]\n17. **Standard for the proportion of threat assessments performed within the required period** (\u00a7 10A-4(a-3)): [___ percent]\n18. **Period within which an action for the publication of covered information must be brought** (\u00a7 10A-5(e)): [___ years after the individual knew or reasonably should have known]\n19. **Period within which a measure available without an assessment shall be provided after request** (\u00a7 10A-4A(a-1)): [___ days]\n20. **Interval at which personal security training shall be offered** (\u00a7 10A-4A(a-1)): [___ months]\n21. **Period within which a case shall reach a disposition, or a report or other behavior be determined to open none** (\u00a7 10A-4B(a)): [___ days]\n22. **Retention period for case information following disposition** (\u00a7 10A-4B(d)): [___ years; and for a case closed under \u00a7 10A-4B(b)(1), or a determination under \u00a7 10A-4B(a) that no case opens, ___ days]\n23. **Maximum period of a monitoring disposition** (\u00a7 10A-4B(e)): [___ days]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-06"}}}};
